window.DOCS_BUNDLE = {"README.md": "# Backend Development — 18722\n\n> **Made by Arnav Daftuar**  \n> Complete documentation hub and interactive web portal for **Backend Development (18722)** coursework — spanning web fundamentals, browser storage, Node.js, Express, EJS, Python, Flask, Django, FastAPI, JWT authentication, MongoDB, PostgreSQL + JSONB, Socket.IO real-time apps, security, and Docker.\n\n[![Made by Arnav Daftuar](https://img.shields.io/badge/Made%20by-Arnav%20Daftuar-4f46e5?style=for-the-badge)](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n[![GitHub Repository](https://img.shields.io/badge/GitHub-Backend.Develepment.18722-181717?style=for-the-badge&logo=github)](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n[![Documentation Website](https://img.shields.io/badge/Website-Live%20Docs%20Portal-0ea5e9?style=for-the-badge&logo=googlechrome&logoColor=white)](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/)\n\n> **View as a Website:** Open [`index.html`](./index.html) locally or visit the **[Backend Development Website Portal](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/)** for a modern, searchable web UI that renders every `README.md` and links directly to interactive demos.\n\n---\n\n## 📌 Quick Navigation\n\n| Section | Coverage | Section README | Website Portal |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **LABS** | Django To-Do App, `EXP-1`, and Lab Tests 1–10 | [LABS/README.md](./LABS/README.md) | [Open Labs in Website](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/README.md) |\n| **Theory** | Assignments 1–2, JWT Lab, Theory 1, Unit 3 & Unit 4 | [Theory/README.md](./Theory/README.md) | [Open Theory in Website](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=Theory/README.md) |\n| **Applications** | Full CMS Lab Web Application | [Applications/README.md](./Applications/README.md) | [Open Apps in Website](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=Applications/README.md) |\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Complete Connected README Directory\n\nEvery `README.md` in this repository is cross-linked to this main hub so you can navigate seamlessly on **GitHub** and in the **Website UI**:\n\n| Category | Project / Module | Folder | Connected `README.md` | Interactive Demo / App |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Root** | Main Repository Hub | [Root](./) | [README.md](./README.md) | [Website Portal](./index.html) |\n| **LABS** | Labs Overview Index | [LABS/](./LABS/) | [LABS/README.md](./LABS/README.md) | — |\n| **LABS** | Django To-Do App | [django_todo_app/](./LABS/django_todo_app/) | [LABS/django_todo_app/README.md](./LABS/django_todo_app/README.md) | Django (`localhost:8000`) |\n| **LABS** | EXP-1 — HTML & CSS Intro | [EXP-1/](./LABS/EXP-1/) | [LABS/EXP-1/README.md](./LABS/EXP-1/README.md) | [arnav.html](./LABS/EXP-1/arnav.html) |\n| **LABS** | Lab Test-1 — Simple CMS Blog | [Lab Test-1 Experiment/](./LABS/Lab%20Test-1%20Experiment/) | [Lab Test-1 README](./LABS/Lab%20Test-1%20Experiment/README.md) | [Lab Report](./LABS/Lab%20Test-1%20Experiment/Report.md) |\n| **LABS** | Lab Test-2 — Responsive Web Page | [Lab Test-2 Experiment/](./LABS/Lab%20Test-2%20Experiment/) | [Lab Test-2 README](./LABS/Lab%20Test-2%20Experiment/README.md) | [index.html](./LABS/Lab%20Test-2%20Experiment/index.html) |\n| **LABS** | Lab Test-3 — Node, Express & EJS | [Lab Test-3 Experiment/](./LABS/Lab%20Test-3%20Experiment/) | [Lab Test-3 README](./LABS/Lab%20Test-3%20Experiment/README.md) | Express (`localhost:3000`) |\n| **LABS** | Lab Test-4 — Express + Mongoose | [Lab Test-4 Experiment/](./LABS/Lab%20Test-4%20Experiment/) | [Lab Test-4 README](./LABS/Lab%20Test-4%20Experiment/README.md) | Express (`localhost:3000`) |\n| **LABS** | Lab Test-5 — PostgreSQL vs MongoDB | [Lab Test-5 Experiment/](./LABS/Lab%20Test-5%20Experiment/) | [Lab Test-5 README](./LABS/Lab%20Test-5%20Experiment/README.md) | [Visual UI](./LABS/Lab%20Test-5%20Experiment/ui/index.html) |\n| **LABS** | Lab Test-6 — Express Middleware | [Lab Test-6 Experiment/](./LABS/Lab%20Test-6%20Experiment/) | [Lab Test-6 README](./LABS/Lab%20Test-6%20Experiment/README.md) | Express (`localhost:3004`) |\n| **LABS** | Lab Test-7 — EJS Templating Engine | [Lab Test-7 Experiment/](./LABS/Lab%20Test-7%20Experiment/) | [Lab Test-7 README](./LABS/Lab%20Test-7%20Experiment/README.md) | Express (`localhost:3005`) |\n| **LABS** | Lab Test-8 — REST API & JWT Auth | [Lab Test-8 Experiment/](./LABS/Lab%20Test-8%20Experiment/) | [Lab Test-8 README](./LABS/Lab%20Test-8%20Experiment/README.md) | Express (`localhost:3007`) |\n| **LABS** | Lab Test-9 — Security, Rate Limit & Docker | [Lab Test-9 Experiment/](./LABS/Lab%20Test-9%20Experiment/) | [Lab Test-9 README](./LABS/Lab%20Test-9%20Experiment/README.md) | Express / Docker (`3009`) |\n| **LABS** | Lab Test-10 — Socket.IO Real-Time Chat | [Lab Test-10 Experiment/](./LABS/Lab%20Test-10%20Experiment/) | [Lab Test-10 README](./LABS/Lab%20Test-10%20Experiment/README.md) | Socket.IO (`localhost:3015`) |\n| **Theory** | Theory Overview Index | [Theory/](./Theory/) | [Theory/README.md](./Theory/README.md) | — |\n| **Theory** | Assignment 1 — NoteSpace (Web Storage) | [Assignment-1-Notes-App/](./Theory/Assignment-1-Notes-App/) | [Assignment 1 README](./Theory/Assignment-1-Notes-App/README.md) | [index.html](./Theory/Assignment-1-Notes-App/index.html) |\n| **Theory** | Assignment 2 — PostgreSQL JSONB Lab | [Assignment 2/](./Theory/Assignment%202/) | [Assignment 2 README](./Theory/Assignment%202/README.md) | [index.html](./Theory/Assignment%202/index.html) |\n| **Theory** | JWT Authentication Lab (FastAPI) | [JWT-Authentication-Lab/](./Theory/JWT-Authentication-Lab/) | [JWT Auth Lab README](./Theory/JWT-Authentication-Lab/README.md) | [Lecture Notes](./Theory/JWT-Authentication-Lab/docs/LECTURE_NOTES.md) |\n| **Theory** | Theory 1 — Express/EJS & Flask | [Theory 1/](./Theory/Theory%201/) | [Theory 1 README](./Theory/Theory%201/README.md) | Express (`3000`) / Flask (`5000`) |\n| **Theory** | Unit 3 — Django Student System | [Unit 3/](./Theory/Unit%203/) | [Unit 3 README](./Theory/Unit%203/README.md) | Django (`localhost:8000`) |\n| **Theory** | Unit 4 — Docker & Containerized Express | [Unit 4/](./Theory/Unit%204/) | [Unit 4 README](./Theory/Unit%204/README.md) | Docker (`localhost:3000`) |\n| **Applications** | Applications Overview Index | [Applications/](./Applications/) | [Applications/README.md](./Applications/README.md) | — |\n| **Applications** | CMS Lab (Express + EJS + MongoDB) | [cms-lab/](./Applications/cms-lab/) | [Applications/cms-lab/README.md](./Applications/cms-lab/README.md) | Express (`localhost:3000`) |\n\n---\n\n## 📂 Repository Structure\n\n```text\nBackend.Develepment.18722/\n│\n├── index.html                        # Modernised documentation & portfolio website\n├── .nojekyll                          # Enables clean static serving on GitHub Pages\n├── assets/\n│   ├── css/site.css                  # Website dark/light theme & responsive styling\n│   └── js/\n│       ├── docs-bundle.js            # Offline-safe embedded Markdown fallback\n│       └── site.js                   # Interactive Markdown renderer, search & router\n│\n├── Applications/\n│   ├── README.md                     # Applications section index\n│   └── cms-lab/\n│       └── README.md                 # Full CMS Lab documentation\n│\n├── LABS/\n│   ├── README.md                     # Complete LABS index\n│   ├── django_todo_app/README.md\n│   ├── EXP-1/README.md\n│   ├── Lab Test-1 Experiment/README.md\n│   ├── Lab Test-2 Experiment/README.md\n│   ├── Lab Test-3 Experiment/README.md\n│   ├── Lab Test-4 Experiment/README.md\n│   ├── Lab Test-5 Experiment/README.md\n│   ├── Lab Test-6 Experiment/README.md\n│   ├── Lab Test-7 Experiment/README.md\n│   ├── Lab Test-8 Experiment/README.md\n│   ├── Lab Test-9 Experiment/README.md\n│   └── Lab Test-10 Experiment/README.md\n│\n├── Theory/\n│   ├── README.md                     # Complete Theory index\n│   ├── Assignment-1-Notes-App/README.md\n│   ├── Assignment 2/README.md\n│   ├── JWT-Authentication-Lab/README.md\n│   ├── Theory 1/README.md\n│   ├── Unit 3/README.md\n│   └── Unit 4/README.md\n│\n├── .gitignore\n└── README.md                         # You are here\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🧪 Laboratory Work Summary\n\nThe [`LABS`](./LABS/README.md) directory tracks the progression from foundational HTML/CSS pages to full middleware pipelines, JWT-protected REST APIs, hardened production servers, Django CRUD, and real-time WebSockets:\n\n1. **[Django To-Do App](./LABS/django_todo_app/README.md)** — Server-rendered task manager using Django MVT, `ModelForm`, CSRF protection, SQLite, and automated tests.\n2. **[EXP-1](./LABS/EXP-1/README.md)** — Semantic HTML5 and responsive CSS card introduction page ([preview](./LABS/EXP-1/arnav.html)).\n3. **[Lab Test-1 Experiment](./LABS/Lab%20Test-1%20Experiment/README.md)** — Simple CMS Blog with Node.js, Express, EJS, and MongoDB.\n4. **[Lab Test-2 Experiment](./LABS/Lab%20Test-2%20Experiment/README.md)** — Zero-framework responsive landing page using Flexbox, CSS Grid, and media queries ([preview](./LABS/Lab%20Test-2%20Experiment/index.html)).\n5. **[Lab Test-3 Experiment](./LABS/Lab%20Test-3%20Experiment/README.md)** — Node.js, Express routing, query/route params, POST handlers, EJS views, and Nodemon.\n6. **[Lab Test-4 Experiment](./LABS/Lab%20Test-4%20Experiment/README.md)** — Express + Mongoose user registration, login, and MongoDB persistence.\n7. **[Lab Test-5 Experiment](./LABS/Lab%20Test-5%20Experiment/README.md)** — PostgreSQL vs MongoDB CRUD comparison with JSONB extensions and [companion UI](./LABS/Lab%20Test-5%20Experiment/ui/index.html).\n8. **[Lab Test-6 Experiment](./LABS/Lab%20Test-6%20Experiment/README.md)** — Custom Express middleware pipeline, auth guards, 404, and centralized error handling.\n9. **[Lab Test-7 Experiment](./LABS/Lab%20Test-7%20Experiment/README.md)** — Server-side rendering with EJS templates, validation, and dynamic student routes.\n10. **[Lab Test-8 Experiment](./LABS/Lab%20Test-8%20Experiment/README.md)** — RESTful Books CRUD API protected with bcrypt + JWT Bearer tokens.\n11. **[Lab Test-9 Experiment](./LABS/Lab%20Test-9%20Experiment/README.md)** — Helmet security headers, CORS allowlists, rate limiting, `/health`, and Docker Compose.\n12. **[Lab Test-10 Experiment](./LABS/Lab%20Test-10%20Experiment/README.md)** — Real-time group chat powered by Express and Socket.IO WebSockets.\n\n---\n\n## 📚 Theory & Units Summary\n\nThe [`Theory`](./Theory/README.md) directory connects client-side state, hybrid SQL/NoSQL modeling, multi-framework APIs, authentication, Django, and Docker:\n\n1. **[Assignment 1 — NoteSpace](./Theory/Assignment-1-Notes-App/README.md)** — Browser-first Notes App built with `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, and JSON ([open app](./Theory/Assignment-1-Notes-App/index.html)).\n2. **[Assignment 2 — PostgreSQL JSONB](./Theory/Assignment%202/README.md)** — Hybrid relational + document design using `jsonb`, GIN indexes, and an [interactive query UI](./Theory/Assignment%202/index.html).\n3. **[JWT Authentication Lab](./Theory/JWT-Authentication-Lab/README.md)** — FastAPI + SQLAlchemy Student Management API with bcrypt hashing, JWT tokens, and RBAC ([lecture notes](./Theory/JWT-Authentication-Lab/docs/LECTURE_NOTES.md)).\n4. **[Theory 1](./Theory/Theory%201/README.md)** — Parallel backend practice servers in Node.js (Express + EJS) and Python (Flask).\n5. **[Unit 3 — Django Student System](./Theory/Unit%203/README.md)** — Django views returning HTTP and JSON responses with URL routing.\n6. **[Unit 4 — Docker & Containers](./Theory/Unit%204/README.md)** — Java container state preservation and a Dockerized Express API (`Dockerfile`).\n\n---\n\n## 🖥️ Applications Summary\n\nThe [`Applications`](./Applications/README.md) directory houses larger integrated projects:\n\n- **[CMS Lab](./Applications/cms-lab/README.md)** — Full-featured Content Management System with Express.js, EJS, responsive CSS, MongoDB persistence, and automatic in-memory demo fallback.\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Technologies Used\n\n| Category | Technologies |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Frontend & Docs UI** | HTML5, CSS3, Responsive Grid/Flexbox, Vanilla JavaScript, LocalStorage, SessionStorage |\n| **Node.js Backend** | Node.js, Express.js, EJS, Nodemon, Socket.IO |\n| **Python Backend** | Python 3, Django, FastAPI, Flask, Uvicorn, Pydantic, Passlib / bcrypt |\n| **Databases & ORM/ODM** | PostgreSQL, JSONB, MongoDB, Mongoose, SQLite, SQLAlchemy |\n| **Auth & Security** | JWT (`jsonwebtoken`, `python-jose`), Bearer tokens, CSRF protection, Helmet, CORS, Rate Limiting |\n| **DevOps & Tooling** | Docker, Docker Compose, Git, GitHub, GitHub Pages |\n\n---\n\n## 🌐 General Backend Architecture\n\n```text\n                 USER / BROWSER\n                       │\n          HTTP Request │ WebSocket\n                       ▼\n           ┌───────────────────────┐\n           │   Backend Framework   │\n           │ Express / Django /    │\n           │ FastAPI / Flask       │\n           └───────────┬───────────┘\n                       │\n         ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐\n         ▼                           ▼\n  Middleware / Auth           Data Layer\n  Logger · Helmet · JWT       MongoDB · PostgreSQL/JSONB · SQLite\n         │                           │\n         └─────────────┬─────────────┘\n                       ▼\n             HTML (EJS/Django) / JSON\n                       │\n                       ▼\n                 Client Browser\n```\n\n---\n\n## ▶️ Running the Website & Projects\n\n### Open the Documentation Website Locally\n\n```bash\n# Serve the root website locally\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen visit `http://localhost:8000` (or open [`index.html`](./index.html) directly in a browser).\n\n### Typical Node.js Project\n\n```bash\nnpm install\nnpm start       # or: npm run dev\n```\n\n### Typical Python / Django / FastAPI Project\n\n```bash\npython3 -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npip install -r requirements.txt\n```\n\nCheck each linked project `README.md` above for exact ports, environment variables (`.env.example`), and database setup instructions.\n\n---\n\n## 👨‍💻 Author\n\n**Made by Arnav Daftuar**  \nB.Tech Computer Science & Engineering · Backend Development (18722)  \nGitHub: [@IamBolt7](https://github.com/IamBolt7) · Repository: [Backend.Develepment.18722](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n", "LABS/README.md": "# 🧪 Backend Development Laboratory (`LABS`)\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../README.md) · [📚 Theory](../Theory/README.md) · [🖥️ Applications](../Applications/README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\nCentral documentation and navigation hub for all practical experiments completed in the **Backend Development** laboratory.\n\n[![Repository](https://img.shields.io/badge/GitHub-Backend.Develepment.18722-181717?logo=github)](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n![Labs](https://img.shields.io/badge/Experiments-12-4f46e5)\n![Made by Arnav Daftuar](https://img.shields.io/badge/Author-Arnav%20Daftuar-0ea5e9)\n\n---\n\n## 🗂️ Complete Lab Index\n\nEvery experiment below links to its folder, detailed `README.md`, and live website documentation view:\n\n| # | Experiment | Focus | Project Folder | Documentation |\n|---:|---|---|---|---|\n| — | **Django To-Do App** | Django CRUD, forms, templates, CSRF, SQLite | [Open Project](./django_todo_app/) | [README](./django_todo_app/README.md) |\n| 1 | **EXP-1** | Semantic HTML5 & CSS3 introduction card | [Open Project](./EXP-1/) | [README](./EXP-1/README.md) |\n| 2 | **Lab Test-1 Experiment** | Simple CMS Blog (Express + EJS + MongoDB) | [Open Project](./Lab%20Test-1%20Experiment/) | [README](./Lab%20Test-1%20Experiment/README.md) |\n| 3 | **Lab Test-2 Experiment** | Responsive web page (Grid, Flexbox, Media Queries) | [Open Project](./Lab%20Test-2%20Experiment/) | [README](./Lab%20Test-2%20Experiment/README.md) |\n| 4 | **Lab Test-3 Experiment** | Node.js, Express routing, params, EJS & Nodemon | [Open Project](./Lab%20Test-3%20Experiment/) | [README](./Lab%20Test-3%20Experiment/README.md) |\n| 5 | **Lab Test-4 Experiment** | Express + Mongoose User Management System | [Open Project](./Lab%20Test-4%20Experiment/) | [README](./Lab%20Test-4%20Experiment/README.md) |\n| 6 | **Lab Test-5 Experiment** | PostgreSQL vs MongoDB & JSONB comparison | [Open Project](./Lab%20Test-5%20Experiment/) | [README](./Lab%20Test-5%20Experiment/README.md) |\n| 7 | **Lab Test-6 Experiment** | Express Middleware Pipeline & Auth Guard | [Open Project](./Lab%20Test-6%20Experiment/) | [README](./Lab%20Test-6%20Experiment/README.md) |\n| 8 | **Lab Test-7 Experiment** | EJS Templating Engine & Dynamic Student Views | [Open Project](./Lab%20Test-7%20Experiment/) | [README](./Lab%20Test-7%20Experiment/README.md) |\n| 9 | **Lab Test-8 Experiment** | RESTful Books API & JWT Authentication | [Open Project](./Lab%20Test-8%20Experiment/) | [README](./Lab%20Test-8%20Experiment/README.md) |\n| 10 | **Lab Test-9 Experiment** | Helmet Security, CORS, Rate Limiting & Docker | [Open Project](./Lab%20Test-9%20Experiment/) | [README](./Lab%20Test-9%20Experiment/README.md) |\n| 11 | **Lab Test-10 Experiment** | Real-time Group Chat with Socket.IO | [Open Project](./Lab%20Test-10%20Experiment/) | [README](./Lab%20Test-10%20Experiment/README.md) |\n\n---\n\n## 🌐 Overall Backend Flow\n\n```text\nUser / Client\n      │\n      ▼\nHTTP Request / WebSocket\n      │\n      ▼\nBackend Application (Express / Django)\n  ├── Middleware (Logger, Helmet, CORS, Rate Limit, Auth Guard)\n  └── Routes & Controllers\n      │\n      ▼\nDatabase / Storage (SQLite / MongoDB / PostgreSQL)\n      │\n      ▼\nHTML Template (EJS / Django) or JSON / Socket Event\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Technology Stack Across Labs\n\n| Area | Technologies / Concepts |\n|---|---|\n| **Frontend** | HTML5, CSS3, Responsive Grid/Flexbox, JavaScript |\n| **JavaScript Backend** | Node.js, Express.js, Nodemon |\n| **Python Backend** | Python 3, Django |\n| **Templating** | EJS, Django Templates |\n| **APIs & Real-Time** | REST, JSON, CRUD, Socket.IO WebSockets |\n| **Auth & Security** | JWT, bcrypt, Bearer tokens, Helmet, CORS, Rate Limiting, CSRF |\n| **Databases** | SQLite, MongoDB, Mongoose, PostgreSQL, JSONB |\n| **Deployment** | `.env` configuration, Docker, Docker Compose |\n\n---\n\n## ▶️ Running Experiments\n\nBecause each experiment uses its own stack and port, check the individual `README.md` linked in the table above.\n\n- **Node.js projects:** `npm install` then `npm start` or `npm run dev`\n- **Django project:** create a `.venv`, `pip install -r requirements.txt`, `python manage.py migrate`, `python manage.py runserver`\n- **Static HTML projects (`EXP-1`, `Lab Test-2`):** open directly in a browser or via the repository [Documentation Website](../index.html)\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../README.md)\n- [📚 Theory README](../Theory/README.md)\n- [🖥️ Applications README](../Applications/README.md)\n- [🌐 Open Interactive Website Portal](../index.html)\n\n---\n\n**Made by Arnav Daftuar** · Backend Development Laboratory\n", "LABS/django_todo_app/README.md": "# ✅ Django To-Do App\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [🧪 LABS README](../README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/django_todo_app/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\nA clean, database-backed **To-Do List application built with Django** for Backend Development practice. The project demonstrates Django models, forms, views, URL routing, templates, static files, CSRF protection, messages, SQLite persistence, and basic automated testing.\n\n## ✨ Features\n\n- Add new tasks with validation\n- View tasks newest-first\n- Mark tasks complete or pending\n- Delete tasks with confirmation\n- Dashboard counters for total, pending, and completed tasks\n- Responsive interface using Django static files\n- CSRF-protected POST actions\n- Friendly 404 behavior for missing task IDs\n- Django Admin integration\n- Automated view tests\n\n## 🧰 Tech Stack\n\n`Python` · `Django` · `SQLite` · `HTML5` · `CSS3`\n\n## 📁 Project Structure\n\n```text\ndjango_todo_app/\n├── todo/\n│   ├── migrations/\n│   ├── static/todo/\n│   │   └── style.css\n│   ├── templates/todo/\n│   │   ├── base.html\n│   │   ├── add_task.html\n│   │   └── task_list.html\n│   ├── admin.py\n│   ├── forms.py\n│   ├── models.py\n│   ├── tests.py\n│   ├── urls.py\n│   └── views.py\n├── todo_project/\n│   ├── settings.py\n│   ├── urls.py\n│   ├── asgi.py\n│   └── wsgi.py\n├── .gitignore\n├── manage.py\n├── requirements.txt\n└── README.md\n```\n\n## 🧠 Django Concepts Demonstrated\n\n| Concept | Implementation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Model | `Task` stores title, completion state, and creation time |\n| ModelForm | `TaskForm` validates and cleans task input |\n| Views | List, add, toggle, and delete task workflows |\n| URL Routing | App URLs map requests to view functions |\n| Templates | Reusable base template and task pages |\n| Static Files | Responsive CSS in `todo/static/todo/` |\n| CSRF | All state-changing forms include CSRF tokens |\n| HTTP Methods | Toggle and delete operations require `POST` |\n| Messages | Success feedback after task actions |\n| Admin | Tasks can be searched and filtered in Django Admin |\n| Tests | Core list/add/toggle/delete behavior is tested |\n\n## 🔄 Application Flow\n\n```text\nBrowser\n   │\n   │ HTTP Request\n   ▼\nDjango URL Router\n   │\n   ▼\nView Function\n   │\n   ├── TaskForm validation\n   │\n   ├── Task model\n   │      │\n   │      ▼\n   │   SQLite DB\n   │\n   ▼\nDjango Template\n   │\n   ▼\nHTML Response\n```\n\n## 🚀 Setup and Run\n\n### 1. Enter the project\n\n```bash\ncd django_todo_app\n```\n\n### 2. Create a virtual environment\n\n```bash\npython3 -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\n```\n\nOn Windows PowerShell:\n\n```powershell\n.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1\n```\n\n### 3. Install dependencies\n\n```bash\npip install -r requirements.txt\n```\n\n### 4. Apply database migrations\n\n```bash\npython manage.py migrate\n```\n\n### 5. Start Django\n\n```bash\npython manage.py runserver\n```\n\nOpen:\n\n```text\nhttp://127.0.0.1:8000/\n```\n\n## 🧪 Run Tests\n\n```bash\npython manage.py test\n```\n\nThe tests cover loading the task list, adding a task, rejecting blank input, requiring POST for task toggling, toggling status, and deleting tasks.\n\n## 🛡️ Improvements Over the Basic Version\n\nThe original classroom version was intentionally minimal. This version keeps the same core task manager while improving its implementation:\n\n- Uses `ModelForm` instead of manually reading raw form input\n- Strips and validates task titles\n- Uses `get_object_or_404()` for safe object retrieval\n- Uses POST instead of GET for toggle/delete actions\n- Adds CSRF protection to state-changing actions\n- Uses `update_fields` for the small toggle update\n- Moves default ordering into the model\n- Adds reusable templates and static CSS\n- Adds user feedback through Django messages\n- Adds automated tests and Django Admin configuration\n- Excludes local virtual environments, caches, SQLite databases, and editor files from Git\n\n## 🗃️ Data Model\n\n```text\nTask\n├── id          Auto-generated primary key\n├── title       CharField(max_length=200)\n├── completed   BooleanField(default=False)\n└── created_at  DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n```\n\n## 🔗 Main Routes\n\n| Method | Route | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `GET` | `/` | Display all tasks |\n| `GET`, `POST` | `/add/` | Show form / create a task |\n| `POST` | `/toggle/<id>/` | Toggle completion state |\n| `POST` | `/delete/<id>/` | Delete a task |\n| `GET` | `/admin/` | Django Admin |\n\n## 🎯 Learning Outcomes\n\nThis project provides practice with Django's MVT architecture, ORM, models, forms, templates, routing, static assets, database migrations, CSRF protection, HTTP method handling, testing, and project organization.\n\n## 👨‍💻 Author\n\n**Arnav Daftuar**  \nBackend Development — Django To-Do Application\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [🧪 Back to LABS README](../README.md)\n- [🌐 Open Website Portal](../../index.html)\n", "LABS/EXP-1/README.md": "# Experiment 1 — Introduction to HTML and CSS\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [🧪 LABS README](../README.md) · [🚀 Open Live Page](./arnav.html) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/EXP-1/README.md)\n\nA simple, responsive personal introduction webpage created as **Experiment 1** for web/backend development practice. The experiment demonstrates the basic structure of an HTML document and the use of CSS to create a clean, responsive user interface.\n\n## Overview\n\nThe project contains a single webpage introducing the student and displaying basic academic information. It is intentionally lightweight and does not require JavaScript, Node.js, a database, or any external framework.\n\nThe improved version focuses on:\n\n- Semantic HTML5 structure\n- Responsive CSS\n- Clean visual hierarchy\n- Reusable CSS variables\n- Mobile-friendly layout\n- Basic accessibility\n- Modern card-based UI\n- Clear source-code organization\n\n## Technologies Used\n\n| Technology | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| HTML5 | Defines the structure and content of the webpage |\n| CSS3 | Handles layout, colors, typography, responsiveness, and visual styling |\n\nNo external libraries or dependencies are required.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```text\nEXP-1/\n├── arnav.html     # Main HTML webpage with embedded CSS\n└── README.md      # Project documentation\n```\n\n## How to Run\n\n### Method 1 — Open directly\n\n1. Download or clone the repository.\n2. Open the `EXP-1` folder.\n3. Double-click `arnav.html`.\n4. The webpage will open in your default browser.\n\n### Method 2 — Visual Studio Code + Live Server\n\n1. Open the project folder in Visual Studio Code.\n2. Install the **Live Server** extension if it is not already installed.\n3. Right-click `arnav.html`.\n4. Select **Open with Live Server**.\n\nA local URL similar to the following will open:\n\n```text\nhttp://127.0.0.1:5500/arnav.html\n```\n\n## Concepts Demonstrated\n\n### HTML Document Structure\n\nThe page uses the standard HTML5 structure:\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  ...\n</head>\n<body>\n  ...\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n### Semantic HTML\n\nThe `<main>` element identifies the primary content of the page. Paragraphs, headings, and a list are used according to their meaning rather than using generic elements everywhere.\n\n### CSS Variables\n\nFrequently used colors are stored as custom properties in `:root`, making the design easier to maintain.\n\n```css\n:root {\n  --bg: #080d1a;\n  --text: #f3f6ff;\n  --accent: #8ec5ff;\n}\n```\n\n### Responsive Design\n\nThe webpage adapts to different screen sizes using flexible widths, `clamp()`, wrapping elements, and a media query. This makes the same page usable on desktop and mobile displays.\n\n### CSS Layout\n\nCSS Grid centers the main card on the screen:\n\n```css\nbody {\n  min-height: 100vh;\n  display: grid;\n  place-items: center;\n}\n```\n\nFlexbox is used for the student-detail badges so they can automatically wrap on smaller displays.\n\n## Page Content\n\nThe webpage displays:\n\n- Student introduction\n- B.Tech CSE program\n- UPES affiliation\n- HTML and CSS as the technologies demonstrated\n- Experiment identification\n\n## Improvements Over the Initial Version\n\nThe original experiment contained a functional single-card introduction page but very little content and no documentation. This version improves it by:\n\n- Adding a proper page description meta tag\n- Using more semantic HTML\n- Improving the introduction text\n- Adding structured student-detail badges\n- Improving typography and spacing\n- Making the design more responsive\n- Adding reusable CSS variables\n- Adding accessibility information to the details list\n- Improving mobile behavior\n- Providing complete project documentation\n- Removing macOS-generated metadata files from the project package\n\n## Learning Outcomes\n\nAfter completing this experiment, a student should be able to:\n\n1. Create a valid HTML5 document.\n2. Use headings, paragraphs, lists, and semantic elements.\n3. Apply CSS rules to HTML elements.\n4. Use colors, spacing, borders, shadows, and typography.\n5. Center content using CSS Grid.\n6. Arrange elements using Flexbox.\n7. Build a webpage that responds to different screen sizes.\n8. Understand the relationship between HTML structure and CSS presentation.\n\n## Possible Future Enhancements\n\nThe experiment can later be extended with:\n\n- A profile image\n- Navigation bar\n- About section\n- Skills section\n- Projects section\n- Contact section\n- External stylesheet\n- CSS animations\n- JavaScript interactions\n- Links to GitHub or a portfolio\n\nThese features are intentionally not required for the current experiment because its main purpose is to demonstrate fundamental HTML and CSS concepts.\n\n## Author\n\n**Arnav Daftuar**  \nB.Tech Computer Science and Engineering  \nUPES\n\n---\n\n> This project is intended for educational and laboratory practice.\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [🧪 Back to LABS README](../README.md)\n- [🚀 Launch Live Webpage (`arnav.html`)](./arnav.html)\n", "LABS/Lab Test-1 Experiment/README.md": "# Lab Test-1 Experiment — Simple CMS Blog\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [🧪 LABS README](../README.md) · [📄 Lab Report](./Report.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/Lab%20Test-1%20Experiment/README.md)\n\n## 1. Project Description\n\nSimple CMS is a simple Blog Content Management System developed using Node.js, Express.js, EJS and MongoDB.\n\nThe application allows users to:\n\n- Create a new blog post\n- View all blog posts\n- View an individual blog post\n- Store blog posts in MongoDB\n- Automatically generate the creation date and time\n\nThe application uses EJS for server-side rendering.\n\n## 2. Technologies Used\n\n- Node.js\n- Express.js\n- EJS\n- MongoDB\n- MongoDB Node.js Driver\n- HTML\n- CSS\n\n## 3. Features\n\n- Create a new blog post\n- View all blog posts\n- View an individual blog post\n- Store posts in MongoDB\n- Automatically generate creation date and time\n- Validate title, content and author\n- Server-side rendering using EJS\n\n## 4. Database\n\nMongoDB is used as the database.\n\n**Connection:** `mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017`\n\n**Database:** `cms_lab`\n\n**Collection:** `posts`\n\n### Post Fields\n\n- `title`\n- `content`\n- `author`\n- `createdAt`\n\nThe `createdAt` field is automatically generated by the backend.\n\n## 5. Installation\n\n### Step 1: Install Node.js\n\nMake sure Node.js is installed.\n\nCheck Node.js:\n\n```bash\nnode --version\n```\n\nCheck npm:\n\n```bash\nnpm --version\n```\n\n### Step 2: Install MongoDB\n\nMake sure MongoDB Server is installed and running.\n\nCheck MongoDB:\n\n```bash\nmongosh --version\n```\n\n### Step 3: Open the Project\n\nOpen the `Lab Test-1 Experiment` folder in VS Code.\n\n### Step 4: Install Dependencies\n\nOpen the terminal in the project folder and run:\n\n```bash\nnpm install\n```\n\n## 6. MongoDB Setup\n\nStart MongoDB and connect using:\n\n```text\nmongodb://127.0.0.1:27017\n```\n\nUse the database:\n\n```text\ncms_lab\n```\n\nThe collection used by the application is:\n\n```text\nposts\n```\n\n## 7. How to Run\n\nMake sure MongoDB is running.\n\nRun the application:\n\n```bash\nnode app.js\n```\n\nThe server will run at:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:3000\n```\n\nOpen this URL in a web browser.\n\n## 8. Routes\n\n| Method | Route | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| GET | `/` | Display all posts |\n| GET | `/posts/new` | Display create-post form |\n| POST | `/posts` | Create a new post |\n| GET | `/posts/:id` | Display an individual post |\n\n## 9. Working\n\n1. Open the home page.\n2. Click **Create New Post**.\n3. Enter the title, content and author.\n4. Click **Create Post**.\n5. The post is stored in MongoDB.\n6. All posts are displayed on the home page.\n7. Click a post title to view the complete individual post.\n\n## 10. Validation\n\nThe application checks that:\n\n- Title is not empty.\n- Content is not empty.\n- Author is not empty.\n\n## 11. Server-Side Rendering\n\nEJS is used for server-side rendering.\n\nThe application contains the following EJS templates:\n\n- `posts.ejs` - Displays all posts\n- `new-post.ejs` - Displays the create-post form\n- `post.ejs` - Displays an individual post\n\n## 12. Project Structure\n\n```text\nLab Test-1 Experiment/\n│\n├── app.js\n├── package.json\n├── package-lock.json\n├── README.md\n├── report.md\n│\n├── views/\n│   ├── posts.ejs\n│   ├── new-post.ejs\n│   └── post.ejs\n│\n└── public/\n    └── style.css\n```\n\n## 13. Testing\n\nThe following features were tested:\n\n- MongoDB connection\n- Creating a new post\n- Storing posts in MongoDB\n- Displaying all posts\n- Viewing an individual post\n- Retrieving a post using MongoDB `_id`\n- Automatic creation date and time\n- Empty field validation\n- EJS server-side rendering\n\n## 14. Result\n\nThe Simple CMS Blog was successfully developed using Node.js, Express.js, EJS and MongoDB.\n\nThe application successfully supports creating, storing, listing and viewing individual blog posts.\n\n## 15. Author\n\n**Saanvi Singh**\n\nB.Tech CSE\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [🧪 Back to LABS README](../README.md)\n- [📄 Full Lab Report](./Report.md)", "LABS/Lab Test-1 Experiment/Report.md": "# Simple CMS Blog - Lab Report\n\n## 1. Title\n\n**Simple CMS Blog - Content Management System**\n\n## 2. Objective\n\nTo design and develop a simple Blog Content Management System using Node.js, Express.js, EJS and MongoDB.\n\nThe system allows users to create blog posts, view all posts and view an individual blog post.\n\n## 3. Technologies Used\n\n- Node.js\n- Express.js\n- EJS\n- MongoDB\n- MongoDB Node.js Driver\n- HTML\n- CSS\n\n## 4. Introduction\n\nA Content Management System (CMS) is a system that allows users to create and manage content.\n\nIn this project, a simple blog CMS has been developed. Users can create blog posts by entering a title, content and author name. The posts are stored in MongoDB and can be viewed through web pages rendered using EJS.\n\n## 5. Database Details\n\nThe application uses MongoDB for data persistence.\n\n### MongoDB Connection\n\n```text\nmongodb://127.0.0.1:27017\n```\n\n### Database\n\n```text\ncms_lab\n```\n\n### Collection\n\n```text\nposts\n```\n\n### Post Fields\n\nEach post contains:\n\n- `title`\n- `content`\n- `author`\n- `createdAt`\n\nThe `createdAt` field is automatically generated by the backend.\n\n## 6. System Features\n\n### 6.1 Create New Post\n\nThe user can create a new blog post by entering:\n\n- Title\n- Content\n- Author\n\nThe form sends the data to the backend using a POST request.\n\n### 6.2 Display All Posts\n\nThe home page retrieves all posts from MongoDB and displays:\n\n- Post title\n- Author\n- Creation date and time\n\nThe post title is clickable.\n\n### 6.3 View Individual Post\n\nWhen the user clicks a post title, the application retrieves the selected post from MongoDB using its unique `_id`.\n\nThe complete post is then displayed on a separate page.\n\n### 6.4 Automatic Date and Time\n\nThe creation date and time are generated automatically by the backend using:\n\n```javascript\nnew Date()\n```\n\nThe user does not enter the creation date manually.\n\n### 6.5 Validation\n\nThe application checks that:\n\n- Title is not empty.\n- Content is not empty.\n- Author is not empty.\n\nIf any required field is empty, the post is not inserted into MongoDB.\n\n## 7. Application Routes\n\n| Method | Route | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| GET | `/` | Display all posts |\n| GET | `/posts/new` | Display create-post form |\n| POST | `/posts` | Create a new post |\n| GET | `/posts/:id` | Display an individual post |\n\n## 8. Project Structure\n\n```text\nLab Test-1 Experiment/\n│\n├── app.js\n├── package.json\n├── package-lock.json\n├── README.md\n├── report.md\n│\n├── views/\n│   ├── posts.ejs\n│   ├── new-post.ejs\n│   └── post.ejs\n│\n└── public/\n    └── style.css\n```\n\n## 9. Working of the Application\n\n### Step 1: Start MongoDB\n\nThe local MongoDB server is started and runs on:\n\n```text\nmongodb://127.0.0.1:27017\n```\n\n### Step 2: Start the Node.js Server\n\nThe application is started using:\n\n```bash\nnode app.js\n```\n\nThe Express server runs on:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:3000\n```\n\n### Step 3: Open the Home Page\n\nThe user opens:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:3000\n```\n\nThe application retrieves the posts from MongoDB and displays them.\n\n### Step 4: Create a Post\n\nThe user clicks **Create New Post**.\n\nThe user enters:\n\n- Title\n- Content\n- Author\n\nAfter submitting the form, the backend validates the fields and stores the post in MongoDB.\n\n### Step 5: View All Posts\n\nAfter successful insertion, the user is redirected to the home page.\n\nThe newly created post is displayed in the list.\n\n### Step 6: View Individual Post\n\nThe user clicks the title of a post.\n\nThe application uses the MongoDB `_id` to retrieve that specific post and displays its complete content.\n\n## 10. Server-Side Rendering\n\nEJS is used as the server-side template engine.\n\nThe following EJS files are used:\n\n### `posts.ejs`\n\nDisplays all blog posts.\n\n### `new-post.ejs`\n\nDisplays the form for creating a new post.\n\n### `post.ejs`\n\nDisplays the complete details of an individual post.\n\n## 11. MongoDB Operations\n\nThe application uses MongoDB operations to store and retrieve posts.\n\n### Insert Post\n\nA new post is inserted into the `posts` collection.\n\nThe data contains:\n\n```text\ntitle\ncontent\nauthor\ncreatedAt\n```\n\n### Retrieve All Posts\n\nThe application retrieves all posts from the collection and displays them on the home page.\n\n### Retrieve Individual Post\n\nThe application retrieves a specific post using its MongoDB `_id`.\n\n## 12. Testing\n\nThe following functionalities were tested:\n\n### Test 1: MongoDB Connection\n\nMongoDB connection was successfully established.\n\nExpected result:\n\n```text\nConnected to MongoDB\n```\n\n### Test 2: Create Post\n\nA test post was created with:\n\n```text\nTitle: My First Blog\n\nContent:\nThis is my first blog post using Node.js, Express, EJS and MongoDB.\n\nAuthor:\nSaanvi Singh\n```\n\nThe post was successfully stored in MongoDB.\n\n### Test 3: Display Posts\n\nThe created post was successfully displayed on the home page.\n\n### Test 4: Individual Post\n\nThe post title was clicked and the complete post was successfully displayed on the individual post page.\n\n### Test 5: Validation\n\nEmpty title, content and author fields were tested.\n\nThe application prevents empty required fields from being submitted.\n\n## 13. Problems Faced During Development and Submission\n\nDuring the development and submission of the Simple CMS Blog project, several technical and setup-related difficulties were faced.\n\n### 13.1 Difficulty in Creating the Correct Project Structure\n\nInitially, there was confusion about where the project files had to be placed.\n\nThe required structure was:\n\n```text\nBackendDevelopment/\n└── Lab/\n    └── Lab Test-1 Experiment/\n```\n\nThe final project structure was:\n\n```text\nLab Test-1 Experiment/\n│\n├── app.js\n├── package.json\n├── package-lock.json\n├── README.md\n├── report.md\n│\n├── public/\n│   └── style.css\n│\n└── views/\n    ├── posts.ejs\n    ├── new-post.ejs\n    └── post.ejs\n```\n\n### 13.2 Difficulty in Setting Up MongoDB\n\nMongoDB was not initially available for the project.\n\nThere was also a problem where the `mongod` command was not recognized in the terminal.\n\nMongoDB Server was then installed and MongoDB Shell (`mongosh`) was used to connect to the local server.\n\nThe local MongoDB connection used was:\n\n```text\nmongodb://127.0.0.1:27017\n```\n\nThe database and collection were created:\n\n```text\nDatabase: cms_lab\nCollection: posts\n```\n\n### 13.3 Confusion Between Local MongoDB and MongoDB Atlas\n\nThere was confusion about whether MongoDB Atlas needed to be used.\n\nThe project was configured to use the local MongoDB server because the lab required the local MongoDB connection.\n\nMongoDB Atlas was therefore not used for the project.\n\n### 13.4 Difficulty in Organizing Code Files\n\nThere was initial confusion about which code belonged in which file.\n\nThe final organization was:\n\n- `app.js` - Express server, routes and MongoDB operations\n- `views/posts.ejs` - Display all posts\n- `views/new-post.ejs` - Create-post form\n- `views/post.ejs` - Display an individual post\n- `public/style.css` - Styling\n\n### 13.5 Confusion Between `post` and `posts`\n\nThere was confusion between the names `post` and `posts`.\n\nThe final naming was:\n\n```text\nposts\n```\n\nfor multiple posts and the MongoDB collection.\n\n```text\npost\n```\n\nfor a single individual post.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nposts.ejs\n```\n\ndisplays multiple posts, while:\n\n```text\npost.ejs\n```\n\ndisplays one post.\n\n### 13.6 Difficulty With the Individual Post Route\n\nThere was confusion about how to open an individual post.\n\nThe route used was:\n\n```text\nGET /posts/:id\n```\n\nThe MongoDB `_id` is used to identify the selected post.\n\nThe application retrieves it using:\n\n```javascript\nnew ObjectId(req.params.id)\n```\n\n### 13.7 Difficulty With Automatic Date and Time\n\nThere was also a need to understand how the creation date should be generated.\n\nThe date and time are not entered by the user.\n\nThey are automatically generated by the backend using:\n\n```javascript\ncreatedAt: new Date()\n```\n\n### 13.8 Difficulty With Git Repository Locations\n\nThere were two different `BackendDevelopment` folders on the computer.\n\nOne was:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\USER\\Downloads\\BackendDevelopment\n```\n\nAnother was:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\USER\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\BackendDevelopment\n```\n\nThe actual project was developed in the Desktop folder.\n\nThis created confusion about which folder was connected to the GitHub repository.\n\nThe correct repository was finally initialized and connected from:\n\n```text\nC:\\Users\\USER\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\BackendDevelopment\n```\n\n### 13.9 Difficulty Connecting the Local Repository to GitHub\n\nThe local project had to be connected to the existing GitHub repository.\n\nThe GitHub repository was:\n\n```text\nhttps://github.com/saanvi-15singh/BackendDevelopment.git\n```\n\nThe local repository was initialized and the GitHub remote was added.\n\nThe branch was configured as:\n\n```text\nmain\n```\n\n### 13.10 Difficulty Creating `.gitignore`\n\nThere was a need to prevent unnecessary files from being uploaded to GitHub.\n\nA `.gitignore` file was created with:\n\n```text\nnode_modules/\n.vscode/\n.env\n```\n\n### 13.11 Unnecessary Files and Folders\n\nThe following files and folders were intentionally excluded:\n\n```text\nnode_modules/\n.vscode/\n.env\n```\n\n#### `node_modules/`\n\nThis folder contains installed Node.js dependencies.\n\nIt does not need to be uploaded because dependencies can be installed again using:\n\n```bash\nnpm install\n```\n\n#### `.vscode/`\n\nThis folder can contain personal VS Code settings and configuration files.\n\nIt was therefore excluded from the repository.\n\n#### `.env`\n\nThis file can contain environment variables and sensitive configuration information.\n\nIt was excluded using `.gitignore`.\n\n### 13.12 Difficulty While Staging Files\n\nThe Git status command was used to check which files were going to be committed.\n\nThis was important to ensure that the actual project files were included while unnecessary files such as `node_modules` were excluded.\n\n### 13.13 Difficulty While Committing\n\nThe project was committed using Git with the commit message:\n\n```text\nAdd Lab Test 1 Simple CMS Blog\n```\n\nDuring the Git process, unusual cleanup prompts related to files inside the `.git/objects` directory appeared.\n\nThe process was interrupted using `Ctrl+C`.\n\nAfterward, `git status` was checked and showed that the working tree was clean.\n\n### 13.14 Difficulty Because Local and Remote Git Histories Were Different\n\nThe local repository and the existing GitHub repository had different histories.\n\nThe local repository contained the newly created project while the GitHub repository already contained previous commits.\n\nThe remote history was merged using:\n\n```bash\ngit merge origin/main --allow-unrelated-histories\n```\n\n### 13.15 Difficulty With the Git Merge Editor\n\nDuring the merge, a Git editor opened with the message:\n\n```text\nMerge remote-tracking branch 'origin/main'\n```\n\nThe merge message was saved and the editor was closed using:\n\n```text\nEsc\n:wq\nEnter\n```\n\nAfter that, the repository was checked using:\n\n```bash\ngit status\n```\n\nThe result was:\n\n```text\nOn branch main\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\n```\n\n### 13.16 Difficulty While Pushing to GitHub\n\nAfter merging the histories, the project was pushed using:\n\n```bash\ngit push -u origin main\n```\n\nThe push was successful.\n\nThe final output confirmed:\n\n```text\n7b7af2a..7d40426  main -> main\nbranch 'main' set up to track 'origin/main'.\n```\n\nThis confirmed that the project was successfully uploaded to GitHub.\n\n## 14. Overall Difficulties Faced\n\nThe main difficulties faced during the project were:\n\n1. Creating the correct folder structure.\n2. Understanding where each code file should be placed.\n3. Setting up MongoDB.\n4. Understanding the local MongoDB connection.\n5. Understanding the difference between MongoDB Atlas and local MongoDB.\n6. Organizing the code files.\n7. Understanding the difference between `post` and `posts`.\n8. Implementing the individual post route using MongoDB `_id`.\n9. Understanding automatic creation date and time.\n10. Finding the correct Git repository folder.\n11. Connecting the correct local repository to GitHub.\n12. Creating and configuring `.gitignore`.\n13. Preventing `node_modules` from being uploaded.\n14. Preventing `.vscode` from being uploaded.\n15. Preventing `.env` from being uploaded.\n16. Understanding Git staging and committing.\n17. Handling different local and remote Git histories.\n18. Handling the Git merge commit editor.\n19. Successfully pushing the merged project to GitHub.\n\n## 15. Learning From the Problems\n\nThe problems faced during the project provided practical understanding of:\n\n- Node.js project organization\n- Express.js routing\n- EJS server-side rendering\n- MongoDB database operations\n- MongoDB ObjectId\n- Form validation\n- Git repository management\n- Git commits\n- Git branches\n- Git remotes\n- Git merge\n- GitHub push\n- `.gitignore`\n- Managing unnecessary project files\n\nThese difficulties helped in understanding the complete development and submission process.\n\n## 16. Result\n\nThe Simple CMS Blog was successfully developed, tested and uploaded to the GitHub repository.\n\nThe final application successfully provides:\n\n- Blog post creation\n- MongoDB data storage\n- Display of all posts\n- Individual post viewing\n- Automatic creation date and time\n- Required-field validation\n- EJS server-side rendering\n- Organized project structure\n- GitHub repository submission\n\n## 17. Conclusion\n\nThe Simple CMS Blog project successfully demonstrates the development of a server-side rendered Content Management System using Node.js, Express.js, EJS and MongoDB.\n\nThe project also provided practical experience in database setup, backend development, project organization, Git version control and GitHub submission.", "LABS/Lab Test-2 Experiment/README.md": "# Lab Test-2 Experiment — Responsive Web Page (HTML & CSS)\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [🧪 LABS README](../README.md) · [🚀 Open Live Page](./index.html) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/Lab%20Test-2%20Experiment/README.md)\n\nA polished, responsive webpage for the **Backend Development Lab**, built entirely with HTML5 and CSS3. The experiment demonstrates semantic HTML, Flexbox, CSS Grid, fluid sizing, and media queries without Bootstrap or another framework.\n\n## Objective\n\nBuild a responsive webpage from scratch that adapts cleanly to desktop, tablet, and mobile screens while demonstrating the viewport meta tag, Flexbox, CSS Grid, and media queries.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```text\nLab Test-2 Experiment/\n├── index.html\n└── README.md\n```\n\n## Technologies\n\n| Technology | Use |\n|---|---|\n| HTML5 | Semantic page structure |\n| CSS3 | Styling and responsive design |\n| Flexbox | Navigation and alignment |\n| CSS Grid | Responsive card layouts |\n| Media Queries | Layout changes at breakpoints |\n| `clamp()` | Fluid typography and spacing |\n\nNo external framework or JavaScript is required.\n\n## Responsive Behaviour\n\n| Screen width | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `> 768px` | Three-column card layout |\n| `561px–768px` | Two-column card layout |\n| `≤ 560px` | Single-column layout and compact navigation |\n| `≤ 360px` | Extra-small phone navigation adjustments |\n\n## Features\n\n- Correct viewport meta tag\n- Semantic HTML5 structure\n- Sticky responsive navigation\n- Modern responsive hero section\n- CSS Grid cards\n- Flexbox navigation\n- Fluid typography and spacing\n- Desktop, tablet, phone, and extra-small-screen handling\n- Hover effects\n- Responsive breakpoint demonstration\n- No horizontal page overflow\n- Clean footer\n- No CSS framework\n\n## Core Concepts\n\n### Viewport\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n```\n\nThis makes the page use the actual device width on mobile browsers.\n\n### CSS Grid\n\nThe desktop card area uses three equal columns:\n\n```css\n.cards {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));\n}\n```\n\n### Media Queries\n\nAt tablet width the grid becomes two columns:\n\n```css\n@media (max-width: 768px) {\n  .cards {\n    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAt phone width it becomes one column:\n\n```css\n@media (max-width: 560px) {\n  .cards {\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## How to Run\n\n1. Extract `Lab Test-2 Experiment.zip`.\n2. Open the extracted folder.\n3. Double-click `index.html`.\n4. The page will open in Safari, Chrome, Firefox, or another modern browser.\n\nThere are no dependencies to install and no server is required.\n\n## How to Test Responsiveness\n\nResize the browser window from desktop width down to phone width. You should see the cards change from **3 → 2 → 1 columns**, while the navigation, typography, and spacing adapt automatically.\n\nYou can also use browser responsive-design tools. In Safari, use **Develop → Enter Responsive Design Mode**. In Chrome, open Developer Tools and enable the device toolbar.\n\nSuggested widths to test: **1440px, 1024px, 768px, 560px, 480px, and 375px**.\n\n## Expected Result\n\nThe page should remain readable and visually balanced across screen sizes without horizontal scrolling. The layout should reorganize rather than simply scale down.\n\n## Learning Outcomes\n\nAfter completing this experiment, you should be able to:\n\n- explain responsive web design;\n- configure the viewport for mobile devices;\n- use Flexbox for flexible alignment;\n- use CSS Grid for responsive multi-column layouts;\n- create and apply media queries;\n- design useful breakpoints for different screen widths;\n- build a webpage without relying on a CSS framework.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThis experiment shows how HTML5 and modern CSS can produce a professional responsive interface. CSS Grid manages the main content layout, Flexbox manages flexible navigation, and media queries adapt the design to the available viewport.\n\n---\n\n**Experiment:** 03 — Create a Responsive Web Page with HTML and CSS  \n**Course:** Backend Development Lab\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [🧪 Back to LABS README](../README.md)\n- [🚀 Launch Live Webpage (`index.html`)](./index.html)\n", "LABS/Lab Test-3 Experiment/README.md": "# Lab Test-3 Experiment — Node.js, Express.js & EJS Templating\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [🧪 LABS README](../README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/Lab%20Test-3%20Experiment/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\n## Experiment 12: Node.js, Express.js, and EJS Templating\n\nA complete Backend Development laboratory experiment demonstrating server-side JavaScript with **Node.js**, web application development with **Express.js**, request/response handling, route and query parameters, POST requests, dynamic pages with **EJS**, and automatic server restart using **Nodemon**.\n\n## Objective\n\nTo understand and implement server-side JavaScript using Node.js; build routes and REST-style endpoints using Express.js; work with HTTP responses, URL parameters, query strings and POST data; generate dynamic HTML using EJS templates; and improve the development workflow using Nodemon.\n\n## Learning Outcomes\n\nAfter completing this experiment, you should be able to:\n\n- Create and execute Node.js programs.\n- Initialize and manage a Node.js project with NPM.\n- Build an Express.js web server.\n- Send text, HTML, JSON and custom-status responses.\n- Read route parameters and query parameters.\n- Process JSON and URL-encoded POST requests.\n- Configure EJS as an Express view engine.\n- Pass server-side data to EJS templates.\n- Render lists and user profiles dynamically.\n- Use Nodemon to restart the development server automatically.\n\n## Technologies Used\n\n| Technology | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| Node.js | JavaScript runtime for the server |\n| NPM | Dependency and script management |\n| Express.js | Routing, middleware and HTTP handling |\n| EJS | Server-side HTML templating |\n| Nodemon | Automatic server restart during development |\n| HTML/CSS | User interface and responsive styling |\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```text\nLab Test-3 Experiment/\n├── app.js                 # Main Express application\n├── script.js              # Basic Node.js example\n├── package.json           # Dependencies and npm scripts\n├── nodemon.json           # Nodemon configuration\n├── public/\n│   └── style.css           # Shared responsive styling\n├── views/\n│   ├── index.ejs           # Experiment dashboard\n│   ├── home.ejs            # Basic EJS example\n│   ├── users.ejs           # Dynamic user list\n│   ├── profile.ejs         # Dynamic profile page\n│   └── result.ejs          # Feedback result page\n└── README.md\n```\n\n## Part A — Node.js and Express Basics\n\n`script.js` demonstrates basic Node.js execution, variables, template literals, arrays and `reduce()`. The Express application then demonstrates multiple response types.\n\n| Method | Route | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GET | `/` | Main experiment dashboard |\n| GET | `/text` | Plain-text response |\n| GET | `/html` | HTML response |\n| GET | `/json` | JSON response |\n| GET | `/status` | Response with HTTP status 201 |\n\n## Part B — URL Parameters, Query Parameters and POST Data\n\n### Route parameters\n\n- `/user/123` reads `123` from `req.params.id`.\n- `/product/electronics/456` reads both category and product ID.\n\n### Query parameters\n\n- `/search?q=nodejs&page=2&limit=20`\n- `/calculate?num1=10&num2=5&operation=add`\n\nThe calculator supports `add`, `subtract`, `multiply`, and `divide`, including division-by-zero handling and input validation.\n\n### POST requests\n\n`POST /register` accepts a JSON body containing `username`, `email`, and `password`. `POST /login` demonstrates simple credential checking for learning purposes.\n\nExample register request:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:3000/register \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"username\":\"john\",\"email\":\"john@example.com\",\"password\":\"pass123\"}'\n```\n\nExample login request:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:3000/login \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"email\":\"test@example.com\",\"password\":\"password123\"}'\n```\n\n> The login credentials and token in this experiment are intentionally hard-coded demonstrations. They are not a production authentication system.\n\n## Part C — EJS Templating\n\nEJS is configured as the Express view engine. The experiment demonstrates interpolation, passing objects and arrays to templates, loops, and dynamic page rendering.\n\n| Route | Template | Demonstrates |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `/home` | `home.ejs` | Dynamic heading, message and server time |\n| `/users` | `users.ejs` | Looping through an array of users |\n| `/profile/1` | `profile.ejs` | Rendering a dynamic user object |\n| `/` | `index.ejs` | Complete interactive experiment dashboard |\n\n## Part D — Nodemon Auto-Restart\n\nNodemon is installed as a development dependency and configured through `nodemon.json`. It watches JavaScript, JSON and EJS files and automatically restarts the server when relevant files change.\n\n```bash\nnpm run dev\n```\n\n## Installation and Execution\n\n### 1. Check Node.js and NPM\n\n```bash\nnode --version\nnpm --version\n```\n\n### 2. Install dependencies\n\nOpen Terminal inside the project directory and run:\n\n```bash\nnpm install\n```\n\n### 3. Run normally\n\n```bash\nnpm start\n```\n\n### 4. Run in development mode\n\n```bash\nnpm run dev\n```\n\n### 5. Open the application\n\nVisit:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:3000\n```\n\nThe home dashboard contains direct links to the GET and EJS demonstrations.\n\n## Testing Checklist\n\n1. Open `/text`, `/html`, `/json`, and `/status` to test response methods.\n2. Open `/user/123` and `/product/electronics/456` to test route parameters.\n3. Open `/search?q=nodejs&page=2&limit=20` to test query parameters.\n4. Open `/calculate?num1=10&num2=5&operation=add` to test the calculator.\n5. Test `/register` and `/login` using curl or Postman.\n6. Open `/home`, `/users`, and `/profile/1` to test EJS rendering.\n7. Submit the feedback form on `/` to test form POST data and server-side rendering.\n8. Run `npm run dev`, edit an EJS or JavaScript file, and observe Nodemon restart the server.\n\n## Important Concepts\n\n### Node.js\n\nNode.js is a JavaScript runtime built on the V8 engine. Its event-driven, non-blocking I/O model makes it useful for network applications and backend services.\n\n### NPM\n\nNPM manages project dependencies and scripts. This project uses it to install Express, EJS and Nodemon and to provide the `start` and `dev` commands.\n\n### Express.js\n\nExpress provides routing and middleware abstractions for Node.js web applications. This experiment uses `app.get()`, `app.post()`, `res.send()`, `res.json()`, `res.status()` and `res.render()`.\n\n### EJS\n\nEJS (Embedded JavaScript) generates HTML on the server using JavaScript values. `<%= value %>` outputs escaped values, while `<% ... %>` executes control-flow code such as loops and conditions.\n\n### Nodemon\n\nNodemon monitors project files during development and restarts the Node.js process after changes, reducing the need for manual restarts.\n\n## Expected Result\n\nThe Express server should start successfully on port `3000`. Each GET endpoint should return the expected response, POST endpoints should process request bodies, and EJS pages should render dynamic data correctly. The interface is responsive for desktop and smaller screens.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThis experiment demonstrates the core workflow of a Node.js backend application: creating a project with NPM, building routes with Express, processing different forms of request data, returning multiple response types, rendering dynamic pages with EJS, and using Nodemon during development. These concepts provide a foundation for larger REST APIs and server-rendered web applications.\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [🧪 Back to LABS README](../README.md)\n- [🌐 Open Website Portal](../../index.html)\n", "LABS/Lab Test-4 Experiment/README.md": "# Lab Test-4 Experiment — Express + Mongoose User Management System\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [🧪 LABS README](../README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/Lab%20Test-4%20Experiment/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\n## Experiment 13A — Express + Mongoose User Management System\n\nA beginner-friendly backend development experiment that demonstrates how **Node.js**, **Express.js**, **MongoDB**, and **Mongoose** work together. The project provides a small browser-based user management system where users can register, log in, and view records stored in MongoDB.\n\n> **Academic note:** This project intentionally keeps authentication simple so the Mongoose workflow is easy to study. Passwords are stored as plain text for demonstration only and this approach must not be used in a production application.\n\n## Aim\n\nTo create an Express application connected to MongoDB through Mongoose and understand schemas, models, document creation, database queries, validation, routing, and basic error handling.\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nAfter completing this experiment, you should be able to:\n\n- connect a Node.js application to MongoDB with Mongoose;\n- define the structure of MongoDB documents using a Mongoose schema;\n- create and use a Mongoose model;\n- insert documents into MongoDB;\n- retrieve one or multiple documents;\n- use asynchronous database operations with `async`/`await`;\n- handle duplicate keys and common application errors;\n- connect HTML forms to Express routes.\n\n## Technology Stack\n\n| Technology | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| Node.js | JavaScript runtime for the backend |\n| Express.js | Web server and routing |\n| MongoDB | NoSQL document database |\n| Mongoose | ODM library between Node.js and MongoDB |\n| HTML/CSS | Simple browser interface |\n\n## Features\n\n- User registration with username, email, and password\n- Unique username and email fields\n- Basic login verification\n- View all registered users\n- Automatic account creation date\n- Mongoose validation\n- Duplicate-key error handling\n- Responsive browser interface\n- Environment-variable support for `PORT` and `DB_URL`\n- Friendly error and 404 pages\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```text\nLab Test-4 Experiment/\n├── server.js       # Express server, schema, model, routes and UI\n├── package.json    # Project information, scripts and dependencies\n├── .gitignore      # Files that should not be committed to GitHub\n└── README.md       # Experiment documentation\n```\n\n`node_modules/` and `package-lock.json` are generated after `npm install`. The dependency folder should not be committed to GitHub.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\nInstall the following before running the experiment:\n\n1. **Node.js** and npm\n2. **MongoDB Community Edition** locally, or access to MongoDB Atlas\n3. A browser such as Safari, Chrome, or Firefox\n4. A code editor such as Visual Studio Code (recommended)\n\nCheck Node.js and npm:\n\n```bash\nnode --version\nnpm --version\n```\n\n## Installation and Setup\n\n### 1. Open the project directory\n\nOn macOS, open Terminal and move into the extracted folder. For example:\n\n```bash\ncd \"/path/to/Lab Test-4 Experiment\"\n```\n\n### 2. Install dependencies\n\n```bash\nnpm install\n```\n\nThis installs Express and Mongoose from `package.json`.\n\n### 3. Start MongoDB\n\nThe default database URL used by the project is:\n\n```text\nmongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/userdb\n```\n\nIf MongoDB was installed through Homebrew, a common command is:\n\n```bash\nbrew services start mongodb-community\n```\n\nAlternatively, use the command appropriate for your MongoDB installation.\n\n### 4. Start the application\n\n```bash\nnpm start\n```\n\nYou can also run:\n\n```bash\nnode server.js\n```\n\nFor Node.js versions that support watch mode, this project also includes:\n\n```bash\nnpm run dev\n```\n\n### 5. Open the application\n\nVisit:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:3000\n```\n\nA successful startup normally prints:\n\n```text\nServer running on http://localhost:3000\nConnected to MongoDB successfully\n```\n\n## How the Application Works\n\n### 1. MongoDB Connection\n\nMongoose establishes the database connection:\n\n```js\nmongoose.connect(DB_URL)\n```\n\nBy default, the application uses the `userdb` database. You can provide another URL through the `DB_URL` environment variable without changing the source code.\n\n### 2. Mongoose Schema\n\nA schema describes the expected structure and validation rules for a document:\n\n```js\nconst userSchema = new mongoose.Schema({\n  username: { type: String, required: true, unique: true },\n  email: { type: String, required: true, unique: true },\n  password: { type: String, required: true },\n  createdAt: { type: Date, default: Date.now }\n});\n```\n\n### 3. Mongoose Model\n\n```js\nconst User = mongoose.model('User', userSchema);\n```\n\nThe `User` model is the interface used to create and query user documents. Mongoose normally maps this model to a MongoDB collection named `users`.\n\n### 4. Express Routes\n\n| Method | Route | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `GET` | `/` | Display registration, login, and user-list controls |\n| `POST` | `/signup` | Validate and save a new user |\n| `POST` | `/login` | Find a user and check the supplied credentials |\n| `GET` | `/users` | Retrieve and display all users |\n\n## Mongoose Methods Covered\n\n| Method | Purpose | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `.save()` | Save a document instance | `await user.save()` |\n| `.create()` | Create and save a document | `await User.create(data)` |\n| `.find()` | Retrieve multiple documents | `await User.find()` |\n| `.findOne()` | Retrieve one matching document | `await User.findOne({ username })` |\n| `.findById()` | Retrieve a document by its ID | `await User.findById(id)` |\n| `.updateOne()` | Update one matching document | `await User.updateOne(filter, update)` |\n| `.deleteOne()` | Delete one matching document | `await User.deleteOne(filter)` |\n\nThe application directly demonstrates `create()`, `findOne()`, and `find()`. The remaining methods are included because they are important CRUD operations covered by the Mongoose topic.\n\n## Testing Procedure\n\n### Test 1 — Register a User\n\n1. Open `http://localhost:3000`.\n2. Enter a username, email, and password.\n3. Select **Create Account**.\n4. A success page should appear.\n5. MongoDB should now contain the new document.\n\n### Test 2 — Duplicate User\n\nTry registering the same username or email again. MongoDB's unique index should cause error code `11000`, and the application displays a readable duplicate-user message.\n\n### Test 3 — Login\n\nEnter the username and password of a registered account. Correct credentials display the user's details; incorrect credentials display a login error.\n\n### Test 4 — View All Users\n\nSelect **View All Users**. The application uses `User.find()` and displays the users stored in the database without displaying passwords.\n\n## Expected Database Document\n\nA user document is conceptually similar to:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"_id\": \"MongoDB-generated ObjectId\",\n  \"username\": \"student01\",\n  \"email\": \"student01@example.com\",\n  \"password\": \"demo-password\",\n  \"createdAt\": \"automatically generated date\"\n}\n```\n\nMongoDB automatically creates `_id`. Mongoose supplies `createdAt` using the schema's default value.\n\n## Common Mongoose Concepts\n\n**Schema:** Defines fields, types, defaults, and validation rules for documents.\n\n**Model:** A JavaScript interface created from a schema and used to interact with a MongoDB collection.\n\n**Document:** One stored record in a MongoDB collection. Each registered user is a document.\n\n**Collection:** A group of related MongoDB documents. This experiment uses the `users` collection.\n\n**Query:** An operation used to find, insert, update, or delete data.\n\n**Async/Await:** Database operations take time and are asynchronous. `await` allows the program to wait for the operation inside an `async` function without using deeply nested callbacks.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n### `MongoDB connection error` / `ECONNREFUSED`\n\nMongoDB is probably not running, or the connection URL is incorrect. Start the MongoDB service and then restart the Node.js application.\n\n### `npm: command not found`\n\nInstall Node.js, reopen Terminal, and verify with `node --version` and `npm --version`.\n\n### `EADDRINUSE: address already in use :::3000`\n\nAnother application is already using port 3000. On macOS you can run the project on another port without editing the file:\n\n```bash\nPORT=3001 npm start\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:3001`.\n\n### Duplicate key / error code `11000`\n\nA username or email already exists. Both fields are marked `unique` in the schema, so MongoDB rejects duplicates.\n\n### Application opens but database actions fail\n\nCheck the Terminal where `npm start` is running. Database and server errors are printed there and usually reveal whether MongoDB is disconnected or validation failed.\n\n## MongoDB Atlas (Optional)\n\nInstead of a local database, obtain a MongoDB Atlas connection string and provide it as `DB_URL`. For example on macOS/Linux:\n\n```bash\nDB_URL=\"your-mongodb-connection-string\" npm start\n```\n\nDo **not** commit a real database username, password, or connection secret to GitHub.\n\n## Security Note\n\nThis lab compares passwords directly because its purpose is to teach Express and Mongoose fundamentals. Real authentication systems should use password hashing (for example, bcrypt or Argon2), sessions or secure tokens, server-side validation, rate limiting, secure cookies, environment variables, and appropriate production security controls.\n\n## Possible Extensions\n\nAfter completing the basic experiment, you can extend it with update/delete routes, password hashing, sessions, stronger validation, separate models/controllers/routes, a frontend template engine, REST APIs, MongoDB Atlas, and automated tests.\n\n## Result\n\nThe experiment successfully demonstrates how an Express application can connect to MongoDB through Mongoose, define a schema and model, save user documents, retrieve data with queries, process HTML forms, and handle common database errors.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThis project provides a foundation for MongoDB-backed Node.js applications. It connects the main concepts—**Express routes → Mongoose model → MongoDB documents**—in a small application that can be run and tested locally.\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [🧪 Back to LABS README](../README.md)\n- [🌐 Open Website Portal](../../index.html)\n", "LABS/Lab Test-5 Experiment/README.md": "# Lab Test-5 Experiment\n## Relational vs Document Databases — PostgreSQL & MongoDB\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [🧪 LABS README](../README.md) · [🚀 Open Companion UI](./ui/index.html) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/Lab%20Test-5%20Experiment/README.md)\n\nA complete Backend Development lab comparing a **relational database (PostgreSQL)** with a **document database (MongoDB)** through the same student-management use case. The experiment also demonstrates how PostgreSQL **JSONB** can provide document-style flexibility inside a relational database.\n\n## Objectives\n\n- Create the `student_management` PostgreSQL database.\n- Create a typed `students` table with `id`, `name`, `branch`, `email`, and `enrollment_date`.\n- Insert at least five student records.\n- Perform CRUD operations and filtering in PostgreSQL.\n- Create a MongoDB `students` collection with at least three documents.\n- Perform equivalent MongoDB CRUD operations.\n- Compare SQL and document-oriented querying.\n- Demonstrate PostgreSQL JSONB, JSON operators, containment queries, and a GIN index.\n- Provide a small responsive browser UI to present the experiment and sample dataset.\n\n## Repository Structure\n\n```text\nLab Test-5 Experiment/\n├── README.md\n├── RUN_ON_MAC.md\n├── comparison.md\n├── postgresql_lab.sql\n├── run_postgresql.sql\n├── mongodb_lab.js\n├── jsonb_extension.sql\n└── ui/\n    ├── index.html\n    ├── styles.css\n    └── app.js\n```\n\n## Experiment Dataset\n\n| ID | Name | Branch | Email | Enrollment Date |\n|---:|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Alice Sharma | CSE | alice@example.com | 2024-01-15 |\n| 2 | Bilal Khan | ECE | bilal@example.com | 2023-08-10 |\n| 3 | Carla Gomez | CSE | carla@example.com | 2024-03-02 |\n| 4 | Divya Nair | ME | divya@example.com | 2023-11-20 |\n| 5 | Ethan Brooks | CSE | ethan@example.com | 2024-02-18 |\n\n## PostgreSQL Implementation\n\nThe schema uses an identity primary key, typed columns, a unique email constraint, and a default enrollment date.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE students (\n    id INTEGER GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,\n    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    branch VARCHAR(50),\n    email VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE,\n    enrollment_date DATE DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE\n);\n```\n\nRequired queries are included in `postgresql_lab.sql` and the repeatable runner `run_postgresql.sql`:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM students WHERE branch = 'CSE';\nSELECT * FROM students WHERE enrollment_date > '2024-01-31';\nUPDATE students SET branch = 'AI/ML' WHERE email = 'bilal@example.com';\nDELETE FROM students WHERE email = 'divya@example.com';\n```\n\nThe SQL portion also includes a case-insensitive search and branch-wise aggregation as bonus operations.\n\n## MongoDB Implementation\n\n`mongodb_lab.js` creates equivalent student documents and demonstrates `insertMany()`, `find()`, `updateOne()`, and `deleteOne()`.\n\n```javascript\ndb.students.find({ branch: \"CSE\" })\ndb.students.find({ enrollment_date: { $gt: new Date(\"2024-01-31\") } })\ndb.students.updateOne(\n  { email: \"bilal@example.com\" },\n  { $set: { branch: \"AI/ML\" } }\n)\ndb.students.deleteOne({ email: \"carla@example.com\" })\n```\n\n## PostgreSQL JSONB Extension\n\n`jsonb_extension.sql` adds a flexible `profile` field while preserving the relational student schema.\n\n```sql\nALTER TABLE students ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS profile JSONB;\n\nUPDATE students\nSET profile = '{\"skills\":[\"python\",\"sql\"],\"clubs\":{\"robotics\":true}}'\nWHERE email = 'alice@example.com';\n```\n\nIt demonstrates `->`, `->>`, `@>` and a GIN index for JSONB lookup.\n\n## Browser UI\n\nA responsive presentation UI is included in `ui/`. It does not replace PostgreSQL or MongoDB; it is a visual companion for the lab. It contains:\n\n- experiment overview and technology cards;\n- searchable/filterable student table;\n- PostgreSQL and MongoDB query examples;\n- relational-vs-document comparison;\n- JSONB explanation;\n- responsive layout for desktop and mobile.\n\nOpen it directly:\n\n```bash\nopen ui/index.html\n```\n\nOr serve it locally:\n\n```bash\ncd ui\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000`.\n\n## Quick Start on macOS\n\nDetailed instructions are in [`RUN_ON_MAC.md`](RUN_ON_MAC.md). The short version is:\n\n```bash\nbrew install postgresql@16\nbrew services start postgresql@16\ncreatedb student_management\npsql student_management -f run_postgresql.sql\npsql student_management -f jsonb_extension.sql\n```\n\nMongoDB:\n\n```bash\nbrew tap mongodb/brew\nbrew install mongodb-community\nbrew services start mongodb-community\nmongosh mongodb_lab.js\n```\n\n## Expected Results\n\n- Initial CSE query: **Alice Sharma, Carla Gomez, Ethan Brooks**.\n- Enrolled after January 2024: **Carla Gomez, Ethan Brooks**.\n- Update: **Bilal Khan** changes from `ECE` to `AI/ML`.\n- PostgreSQL delete: **Divya Nair** is removed.\n- MongoDB script demonstrates equivalent document CRUD operations.\n- JSONB query returns Alice's skills/profile and can match the nested robotics membership.\n\n## PostgreSQL vs MongoDB Summary\n\n| Feature | PostgreSQL | MongoDB |\n|---|---|---|\n| Model | Tables and rows | Collections and documents |\n| Schema | Explicit and strongly typed | Flexible document shape |\n| Query language | SQL | MongoDB Query Language |\n| Relationships | Native joins + foreign keys | Embedding/references + `$lookup` |\n| Transactions | Full ACID support | Supports multi-document transactions |\n| Flexible fields | JSON/JSONB | Native BSON documents |\n| Good fit here | Core structured student data | Variable/nested student metadata |\n\nFor the complete discussion, see [`comparison.md`](comparison.md).\n\n## Useful `psql` Commands\n\n```text\n\\l                       list databases\n\\c student_management    connect to the database\n\\dt                      list tables\n\\d students              describe the students table\n\\du                      list roles/users\n\\?                       psql command help\n\\h CREATE TABLE          SQL syntax help\n\\i /path/to/file.sql     run a SQL file\n\\q                       quit\n```\n\nBackup and restore:\n\n```bash\npg_dump student_management > backup.sql\npsql student_management < backup.sql\n```\n\n## Learning Outcome\n\nThis experiment shows that PostgreSQL is a natural fit when the main data has a predictable structure and benefits from constraints, transactions, and relationships. MongoDB offers convenient document flexibility when records can vary significantly. PostgreSQL JSONB provides a hybrid approach: structured relational columns for core data and flexible JSON for optional or nested attributes.\n\n## Submission Notes\n\nRun the scripts locally and capture genuine terminal output/screenshots if your instructor requires execution evidence. The repository contains executable source files rather than fabricated result screenshots.\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [🧪 Back to LABS README](../README.md)\n- [🚀 Launch Companion Web UI](./ui/index.html)\n- [📘 Mac Setup Guide](./RUN_ON_MAC.md) · [📊 Detailed Comparison](./comparison.md)\n", "LABS/Lab Test-5 Experiment/comparison.md": "# PostgreSQL vs MongoDB — Lab Comparison\n\n| Aspect | PostgreSQL | MongoDB |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data model | Relational tables with rows and typed columns | Collections containing BSON/JSON-like documents |\n| Schema | Strong, explicit schema by default | Flexible document structure |\n| Insert style | `INSERT INTO ... VALUES ...` | `insertOne()` / `insertMany()` |\n| Query style | Standard SQL with `SELECT`, `WHERE`, `JOIN`, etc. | MongoDB Query Language with `find()`, operators, and aggregation pipelines |\n| Update | `UPDATE ... SET ... WHERE ...` | `updateOne()` / `updateMany()` with operators such as `$set` |\n| Delete | `DELETE FROM ... WHERE ...` | `deleteOne()` / `deleteMany()` |\n| Indexing | B-tree and many other index types; GIN is useful for JSONB | `_id` is indexed by default; secondary indexes can be added to fields |\n| Transactions | Strong ACID transaction support across relational data | Supports multi-document transactions; document modeling often reduces the need for cross-document operations |\n| Relationships | Native joins and constraints such as foreign keys | Documents are often embedded; `$lookup` can combine collections |\n| Flexible data | `JSONB` can store and index flexible/nested fields alongside typed columns | Flexible/nested documents are a core part of the model |\n\n## Experience of inserting and querying\nPostgreSQL requires the table structure and data types to be defined first. This makes the data consistent and gives SQL a clear, structured query model. Inserting multiple rows is straightforward, and filters such as branch or enrollment date are concise with `WHERE` conditions.\n\nMongoDB feels more flexible because each student is inserted as a document and the collection does not require the same rigid column definition first. Queries are expressed as document-shaped filters, for example `{ branch: \"CSE\" }`. This is convenient when records may contain different optional or nested fields.\n\nFor this student-management lab, PostgreSQL is well suited to the core student fields because they have a predictable structure. A `JSONB` column can hold optional data such as skills, clubs, metadata, or settings while retaining relational features. MongoDB remains useful when the application is naturally document-shaped and the structure varies substantially between records.\n\n## JSONB takeaway\nPostgreSQL `JSONB` stores JSON in a decomposed binary representation that supports indexing and JSON operators. In this lab, adding a `profile JSONB` column provides document-style flexibility without creating a second database system. The GIN index in `jsonb_extension.sql` demonstrates how JSONB content can be indexed for efficient containment queries.\n", "LABS/Lab Test-5 Experiment/RUN_ON_MAC.md": "# Running Lab Test-5 on macOS\n\n## PostgreSQL\n\nInstall and start PostgreSQL 16:\n\n```bash\nbrew install postgresql@16\nbrew services start postgresql@16\npg_isready\npsql --version\n```\n\nCreate the database once and run the repeatable lab script:\n\n```bash\ncreatedb student_management\npsql student_management -f run_postgresql.sql\npsql student_management -f jsonb_extension.sql\n```\n\nIf `student_management` already exists, skip `createdb`.\n\nInspect it interactively:\n\n```bash\npsql student_management\n```\n\nUseful commands inside `psql`:\n\n```text\n\\dt\n\\d students\nSELECT * FROM students ORDER BY id;\n\\q\n```\n\n## MongoDB\n\nInstall and start MongoDB Community Edition:\n\n```bash\nbrew tap mongodb/brew\nbrew install mongodb-community\nbrew services start mongodb-community\nmongosh --version\n```\n\nRun the lab script:\n\n```bash\nmongosh mongodb_lab.js\n```\n\nOr open `mongosh` and paste the commands from `mongodb_lab.js`.\n\n## Browser UI\n\nThe UI is a presentation layer for the experiment and uses the same sample dataset.\n\n```bash\nopen ui/index.html\n```\n\nFor a local web server:\n\n```bash\ncd ui\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen visit `http://localhost:8000`.\n\n## Recommended evidence for submission\n\nCapture your own terminal output for database creation, inserted rows, CSE query, enrollment-date query, branch update, deletion, MongoDB operations, and JSONB queries. This ensures the submitted screenshots demonstrate your own successful execution.\n", "LABS/Lab Test-6 Experiment/README.md": "# 🚀 Lab Test-6 Experiment — Express Middleware\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [🧪 LABS README](../README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/Lab%20Test-6%20Experiment/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\n> A practical **Express.js middleware experiment** covering the complete request-processing pipeline — from logging and body parsing to authentication, 404 handling, and centralized error management.\n\n---\n\n\n## 📌 Overview\nDemonstrates the full Express middleware pipeline: **custom logger**, **body parser**, **static file serving**, **route-level auth guard**, **404 handler**, and **central error handler**.\n\n## 🧠 Concepts Covered\n| Middleware Type | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Logger | Logs method + URL + timestamp for every request |\n| `express.json()` | Parses JSON request bodies |\n| `express.urlencoded()` | Parses HTML form POST bodies |\n| `express.static()` | Serves files from `public/` folder |\n| Auth Guard | Checks `x-auth-token` header before protected routes |\n| 404 Handler | Catches unmatched routes |\n| Error Handler | 4-param `(err,req,res,next)` catches all thrown errors |\n\n## 📂 Project Files\n- [`app.js`](app.js) – All middleware + routes in one file\n- [`package.json`](package.json) – Dependencies and npm scripts\n- [`.gitignore`](.gitignore) – Excludes dependencies, environment files and OS artifacts\n\n## ⚙️ How to Run\n```bash\ncd \"Lab Test-6 Experiment\"\nnpm install\nnpm run dev\n```\nServer starts on **http://localhost:3004** by default. Set `PORT` to override it.\n\n## 🧪 Test the Routes\n```bash\n# Public route (no token needed)\ncurl http://localhost:3004/public-data\n\n# Protected route – without token (401)\ncurl http://localhost:3004/private-data\n\n# Protected route – with token (200)\ncurl -H \"x-auth-token: secret123\" http://localhost:3004/private-data\n\n# POST echo\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:3004/echo -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d \"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Satyam\\\"}\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🔄 Middleware Pipeline\n\n```text\nClient Request\n      │\n      ▼\nCustom Logger\n      │\n      ▼\nBody Parsers\n(JSON + URL Encoded)\n      │\n      ▼\nStatic File Middleware\n      │\n      ▼\nRoute Matching\n      │\n      ├── Public Route\n      │\n      └── Protected Route\n              │\n              ▼\n           Auth Guard\n              │\n              ▼\n         Route Handler\n              │\n              ▼\n        HTTP Response\n\nUnmatched Route → 404 Handler\nApplication Error → Central Error Handler\n```\n\n## 🔐 Authentication Flow\n\nThe protected route checks the `x-auth-token` request header before allowing access:\n\n```text\n/private-data\n      │\n      ▼\nCheck x-auth-token\n      │\n ┌────┴────┐\n │         │\nInvalid   Valid\n │         │\n ▼         ▼\n401      next()\n           │\n           ▼\n      Private Route\n           │\n           ▼\n         200 OK\n```\n\n> The `secret123` token used in this experiment is suitable for demonstrating middleware behavior only. Production applications should use a proper authentication mechanism.\n\n## 🎯 Learning Outcomes\n\nAfter completing this experiment, you can demonstrate:\n\n- Custom Express middleware\n- Middleware execution order\n- The purpose of `next()`\n- JSON and URL-encoded body parsing\n- Static file serving\n- Route-level authentication\n- Public and protected endpoints\n- `401 Unauthorized` responses\n- `404 Not Found` handling\n- Centralized Express error handling\n- API testing using cURL\n\n## ✅ Experiment Checklist\n\n- [x] Custom request logger\n- [x] JSON body parser\n- [x] URL-encoded body parser\n- [x] Static file middleware\n- [x] Public route\n- [x] Protected route\n- [x] Authentication guard\n- [x] 404 handler\n- [x] Central error handler\n- [x] cURL route testing\n\n## 💡 Key Takeaway\n\nExpress middleware forms a **request-processing pipeline**. Each middleware function can inspect the request, modify the request or response, terminate processing, or call `next()` to pass control to the next middleware.\n\nThis experiment brings those pieces together in one compact application covering **logging, parsing, static files, route protection, not-found handling, and error management**.\n\n---\n\n## 👨‍💻 Author\n\n**Arnav Daftuar**\n\nBackend Development — Lab Test-6 Express Middleware Experiment\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [🧪 Back to LABS README](../README.md)\n- [🌐 Open Website Portal](../../index.html)\n", "LABS/Lab Test-7 Experiment/README.md": "# 🎨 Lab Test-7 Experiment — EJS Templating Engine\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [🧪 LABS README](../README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/Lab%20Test-7%20Experiment/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\n> A server-side rendering project using **Express.js + EJS** to build dynamic student pages, reusable templates, forms, and route-driven views.\n\n## 📌 Overview\nThis experiment demonstrates EJS output tags, JavaScript logic inside templates, dynamic routing, form submission, static CSS, and custom 404 rendering.\n\n## ✨ Features\n| Feature | Implementation |\n|---|---|\n| Dynamic output | `<%= %>` |\n| Loops / conditions | `<% %>` |\n| Server-side rendering | `res.render()` |\n| Student list | `/students` |\n| Student details | `/student/:id` |\n| Add-student form | `GET/POST /form` |\n| Validation | Marks must be 0–100; name/branch required |\n| 404 page | Custom `404.ejs` |\n\n## 📂 Structure\n```text\nLab Test-7 Experiment/\n├── app.js\n├── package.json\n├── .gitignore\n├── public/\n│   └── style.css\n└── views/\n    ├── index.ejs\n    ├── students.ejs\n    ├── student-detail.ejs\n    ├── form.ejs\n    └── 404.ejs\n```\n\n## ▶️ Run\n```bash\ncd \"Lab Test-7 Experiment\"\nnpm install\nnpm run dev\n```\nOpen **http://localhost:3005**.\n\n## 🧭 Routes\n| Method | Route | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| GET | `/` | Home page |\n| GET | `/students` | List all students |\n| GET | `/student/:id` | Student details |\n| GET | `/form` | Add-student form |\n| POST | `/form` | Validate and add a student |\n\n## 🎯 Learning Outcomes\n- Configure EJS as an Express view engine\n- Pass server data into templates\n- Use loops and conditions in EJS\n- Process HTML forms on the server\n- Build dynamic parameterized routes\n- Serve static CSS\n- Validate user input and render errors\n\n## 👨‍💻 Author\n**Arnav Daftuar**  \nBackend Development — Lab Test-7\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [🧪 Back to LABS README](../README.md)\n- [🌐 Open Website Portal](../../index.html)\n", "LABS/Lab Test-8 Experiment/README.md": "# 🔐 Lab Test-8 Experiment — RESTful API + JWT Authentication\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [🧪 LABS README](../README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/Lab%20Test-8%20Experiment/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\n> A combined **RESTful Books CRUD API** and **JWT authentication** exercise built with Express.\n\n## Summary\n- Builds a RESTful CRUD API for a `Books` resource with query-string filtering.\n- Adds JWT login and Bearer-token protection for write operations and user identity.\n\n## API Routes\n\n### 🔑 Authentication\n| Method | Route | Auth | Description |\n|--------|-------|------|-------------|\n| POST | `/auth/login` | Public | Login with username & password, receive JWT token |\n| GET | `/auth/me` | 🔒 Protected | View logged-in user info from token |\n\n### 📚 Books CRUD\n| Method | Route | Auth | Description |\n|--------|-------|------|-------------|\n| GET | `/api/books` | Public | List all books (supports `?genre=Tech&author=Martin`) |\n| GET | `/api/books/:id` | Public | Get single book by ID |\n| POST | `/api/books` | 🔒 Protected | Create new book |\n| PUT | `/api/books/:id` | 🔒 Protected | Update existing book |\n| DELETE | `/api/books/:id` | 🔒 Protected | Delete a book |\n\n## Files\n```\nLab Test-8 Experiment/\n├── server.js        ← Combined REST API + JWT Auth\n├── package.json\n└── .env.example     ← Copy to .env before running\n```\n\n## How to Run\n```bash\ncd \"Lab Test-8 Experiment\"\ncp .env.example .env\nnpm install\nnpm run dev\n```\nServer on **http://localhost:3007**\n\n## Test with curl\n\n```bash\n# 1. Login and get token\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:3007/auth/login \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d \"{\\\"username\\\":\\\"satyam\\\",\\\"password\\\":\\\"password123\\\"}\"\n\n# 2. Use the token for protected routes\ncurl -H \"Authorization: Bearer <YOUR_TOKEN>\" http://localhost:3007/auth/me\n\n# 3. Get all books (public)\ncurl http://localhost:3007/api/books\n\n# 4. Create a book (protected)\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:3007/api/books \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer <YOUR_TOKEN>\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d \"{\\\"title\\\":\\\"Node.js Design Patterns\\\",\\\"author\\\":\\\"Casciaro\\\",\\\"genre\\\":\\\"Tech\\\",\\\"year\\\":2020}\"\n\n# 5. Delete a book (protected)\ncurl -X DELETE http://localhost:3007/api/books/1 \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer <YOUR_TOKEN>\"\n```\n\n## Expected Output\n\n**Login response:**\n```json\n{\n  \"message\": \"Login successful\",\n  \"token\": \"eyJhbGci...\",\n  \"expiresIn\": \"1h\"\n}\n```\n\n**GET /api/books:**\n```json\n{\n  \"count\": 3,\n  \"books\": [\n    { \"id\": 1, \"title\": \"The Pragmatic Programmer\", \"author\": \"Hunt & Thomas\", \"genre\": \"Tech\", \"year\": 1999 },\n    { \"id\": 2, \"title\": \"Clean Code\", \"author\": \"Robert Martin\", \"genre\": \"Tech\", \"year\": 2008 },\n    { \"id\": 3, \"title\": \"You Don't Know JS\", \"author\": \"Kyle Simpson\", \"genre\": \"Tech\", \"year\": 2015 }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n\n## 🎯 Learning Outcomes\n- Design RESTful CRUD endpoints\n- Filter resources with query parameters\n- Hash and verify passwords with bcrypt\n- Issue and verify JWT access tokens\n- Protect routes with Bearer authentication\n- Keep signing secrets in environment variables\n\n## 👨‍💻 Author\n**Arnav Daftuar**  \nBackend Development — Lab Test-8\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [🧪 Back to LABS README](../README.md)\n- [🌐 Open Website Portal](../../index.html)\n", "LABS/Lab Test-9 Experiment/README.md": "# 🛡️ Lab Test-9 Experiment — Security, Rate Limiting & Deployment\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [🧪 LABS README](../README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/Lab%20Test-9%20Experiment/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\n> An Express server demonstrating environment-based configuration, HTTP security headers, CORS, rate limiting, health checks, and Docker packaging.\n\n## Summary\n\n| Exp | Topic | Implementation |\n|-----|-------|----------------|\n| **Configuration** | Environment & Security | `dotenv`, `helmet` (11 security headers), `cors` with allowlist |\n| **Protection** | Rate Limiting | `express-rate-limit` – global (100/15min) + strict auth (5/15min) |\n| **Deployment** | Deployment | `Dockerfile`, `docker-compose.yml`, `/health` endpoint |\n\n## Files\n```\nLab Test-9 Experiment/\n├── server.js           ← Main Express server (Exp09+10+11 combined)\n├── package.json\n├── .env.example        ← Copy to .env before running\n├── Dockerfile          ← Docker image definition\n├── docker-compose.yml  ← Spins up API + MongoDB containers\n└── .gitignore          ← Excludes local secrets and dependencies\n```\n\n## How to Run (Local)\n```bash\ncd \"Lab Test-9 Experiment\"\ncp .env.example .env\nnpm install\nnpm run dev\n```\nServer on **http://localhost:3009**\n\n## How to Run (Docker)\n```bash\n# Build and start both API and MongoDB containers\ndocker compose up --build\n\n# Stop containers\ndocker compose down\n```\n\n## Endpoints\n| Method | Route | Description |\n|--------|-------|-------------|\n| GET | `/` | Feature overview |\n| GET | `/health` | Health check (uptime, env, timestamp) |\n| GET | `/headers` | View applied security headers |\n| POST | `/auth/login` | Rate-limited login (max 5 attempts / 15 min) |\n\n## Expected Output\n\n**GET /health:**\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"OK\",\n  \"env\": \"development\",\n  \"uptime\": \"42s\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-26T04:00:00.000Z\"\n}\n```\n\n**Too many requests (rate limit hit):**\n```json\n{ \"error\": \"Too many requests – please wait 15 minutes and try again.\" }\n```\n\n**Security headers added by helmet (visible in browser DevTools → Network → Response Headers):**\n```\nContent-Security-Policy: ...\nX-DNS-Prefetch-Control: off\nX-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN\nX-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\nReferrer-Policy: no-referrer\n...\n```\n\n\n## 🔒 Security Notes\n- Keep `.env` out of Git; commit only `.env.example`.\n- Helmet applies defensive HTTP headers.\n- CORS is restricted to configured origins.\n- Global and login-specific rate limits reduce abusive request volume.\n\n## 🎯 Learning Outcomes\n- Configure applications with environment variables\n- Apply Helmet and CORS middleware\n- Implement global and route-specific rate limiting\n- Expose a deployment health endpoint\n- Containerize a Node.js service with Docker\n\n## 👨‍💻 Author\n**Arnav Daftuar**  \nBackend Development — Lab Test-9\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [🧪 Back to LABS README](../README.md)\n- [🌐 Open Website Portal](../../index.html)\n", "LABS/Lab Test-10 Experiment/README.md": "# 💬 Lab Test-10 Experiment — Real-time Chat with Socket.IO\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [🧪 LABS README](../README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=LABS/Lab%20Test-10%20Experiment/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\n## Summary\nA fully functional **real-time group chat application** using `socket.io` and Express. Demonstrates WebSocket-based bidirectional communication, user sessions, online user list, system notifications (join/leave), and a styled dark-themed chat UI.\n\n## Concepts Covered\n| Concept | Implementation |\n|---|---|\n| WebSocket via Socket.IO | `io.on('connection', ...)` |\n| Broadcast to all clients | `io.emit('message', payload)` |\n| Broadcast except sender | `socket.broadcast.emit(...)` |\n| Room-less group chat | All sockets share one namespace |\n| Online user tracking | `users` object keyed by `socket.id` |\n| Disconnect cleanup | `socket.on('disconnect', ...)` |\n\n## Files\n```\nLab Test-10 Experiment/\n├── server.js           ← Socket.IO + Express server\n├── package.json\n├── .gitignore\n└── public/\n    └── index.html      ← Full chat UI (HTML + CSS + Socket.IO client)\n```\n\n## How to Run\n```bash\ncd \"Lab Test-10 Experiment\"\nnpm install\nnpm run dev\n```\nOpen **http://localhost:3015** in **two or more browser tabs** to see real-time messaging!\n\n## How Socket.IO Works\n```\nClient A                Server               Client B\n   |──── join(\"Satyam\") ──►|                     |\n   |                       |──── system msg ─────►|\n   |──── message(\"Hello\") ─►|                     |\n   |                       |──── message ─────────►|\n   |◄──────────────────────|──── message           |\n```\n\n\n## ✨ Features\n- Real-time bidirectional messaging\n- Join/leave system notifications\n- Live online-user list\n- Username normalization and message length limits\n- Responsive browser chat interface\n\n## 🎯 Learning Outcomes\n- Create an HTTP server shared with Socket.IO\n- Handle socket connection and disconnect events\n- Broadcast events to all clients or everyone except the sender\n- Track connected users by `socket.id`\n- Build a real-time UI without page refreshes\n\n## 👨‍💻 Author\n**Arnav Daftuar**  \nBackend Development — Lab Test-10\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [🧪 Back to LABS README](../README.md)\n- [🌐 Open Website Portal](../../index.html)\n", "Theory/README.md": "# 📚 Theory — Backend Development\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../README.md) · [🧪 LABS](../LABS/README.md) · [🖥️ Applications](../Applications/README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=Theory/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\nThis directory contains the **Theory assignments, unit exercises, and backend-development practice projects** completed for Backend Development (18722).\n\n---\n\n## 🗂️ Theory Modules Index\n\n| No. | Project / Unit | Main Topic | Folder | Documentation |\n|---:|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Assignment 1 — NoteSpace** | Notes App with `localStorage`, `sessionStorage` & JSON | [Open](./Assignment-1-Notes-App/) | [README](./Assignment-1-Notes-App/README.md) |\n| 2 | **Assignment 2 — PostgreSQL JSONB** | PostgreSQL as SQL + NoSQL using `jsonb` | [Open](./Assignment%202/) | [README](./Assignment%202/README.md) |\n| 3 | **JWT Authentication Lab** | FastAPI Student API with bcrypt & JWT auth | [Open](./JWT-Authentication-Lab/) | [README](./JWT-Authentication-Lab/README.md) |\n| 4 | **Theory 1** | Parallel Node.js (Express + EJS) & Python (Flask) servers | [Open](./Theory%201/) | [README](./Theory%201/README.md) |\n| 5 | **Unit 3 — Django** | Django `student_system` project & JSON endpoints | [Open](./Unit%203/) | [README](./Unit%203/README.md) |\n| 6 | **Unit 4 — Docker & Express** | Container state preservation & Dockerized Express API | [Open](./Unit%204/) | [README](./Unit%204/README.md) |\n\n---\n\n## 🌐 Progression Across Theory\n\n```text\nClient-Side Persistence     ──► Hybrid SQL + NoSQL    ──► Multi-Stack Backends\n(Assignment 1: Web Storage)     (Assignment 2: JSONB)     (Theory 1: Express & Flask)\n                                                                 │\n                                                                 ▼\nContainerization & Deployment ◄── Frameworks & Routing  ◄── API Security & Auth\n(Unit 4: Docker & Express)       (Unit 3: Django)          (JWT Lab: FastAPI + JWT)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🔄 Quick Comparison\n\n| Area | Assignment 1 | Assignment 2 | JWT Auth Lab | Theory 1 | Unit 3 | Unit 4 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Focus** | Browser Storage | PostgreSQL JSONB | API Auth & RBAC | Express & Flask | Django MVT | Docker |\n| **Language** | JavaScript | SQL / JS | Python | JS & Python | Python | Java & JS |\n| **Framework** | Vanilla Web | Vanilla UI + SQL | FastAPI | Express / Flask | Django | Express |\n| **Database / Storage** | `localStorage` / `sessionStorage` | PostgreSQL (`jsonb`) | SQLite + SQLAlchemy | In-memory | SQLite | In-memory |\n| **Interactive UI** | [Open App](./Assignment-1-Notes-App/index.html) | [Open UI](./Assignment%202/index.html) | Swagger `/docs` | EJS / Flask HTML | HTTP / JSON | HTTP / JSON |\n\n---\n\n## ▶️ Running the Modules\n\n- **Static Interactive UIs:** Open [`Assignment-1-Notes-App/index.html`](./Assignment-1-Notes-App/index.html) or [`Assignment 2/index.html`](./Assignment%202/index.html) directly in a browser.\n- **Python Projects (`JWT-Authentication-Lab`, `Theory 1` Flask, `Unit 3` Django):** Create a virtual environment, install dependencies, and run `uvicorn` / `python3` / `manage.py runserver`.\n- **Node.js & Docker (`Theory 1` Express, `Unit 4` Express):** Install with `npm install` or build via `docker build`.\n\nSee each module's linked `README.md` above for exact instructions.\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../README.md)\n- [🧪 LABS README](../LABS/README.md)\n- [🖥️ Applications README](../Applications/README.md)\n- [🌐 Open Interactive Website Portal](../index.html)\n\n---\n\n**Made by Arnav Daftuar** · Backend Development Theory\n", "Theory/Assignment-1-Notes-App/README.md": "# NoteSpace — Web Storage Notes App\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [📚 Theory README](../README.md) · [🚀 Open Live App](./index.html) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=Theory/Assignment-1-Notes-App/README.md)\n\n> **Backend Development · Theory · Assignment 01**  \n> Final Task: Build a Notes App using `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, and JSON.\n\n## Overview\n\n**NoteSpace** is a responsive, browser-based notes application built with plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. The project turns the Web Storage API tutorial into a complete practical application: saved notes persist in `localStorage`, unfinished editor content is preserved in `sessionStorage`, and JavaScript objects are serialized with JSON before storage.\n\nNo framework, backend, database, package manager, or account is required.\n\n## Assignment objectives\n\nThis project demonstrates how to:\n\n- use `localStorage.setItem()`, `getItem()`, `removeItem()`, `key()`, and `length`;\n- use `sessionStorage` for temporary, tab-scoped state;\n- convert objects to strings with `JSON.stringify()`;\n- restore strings with `JSON.parse()` and handle invalid JSON safely;\n- implement CRUD operations using browser storage;\n- build search, category filtering, pinning, import/export, and theme persistence;\n- create a responsive interface using semantic HTML and modern CSS.\n\n## Main features\n\n### Notes workspace\n\n- Create notes with a **title, category, and content**.\n- Edit existing notes without creating duplicates.\n- Delete notes with confirmation.\n- Pin important notes to the top of the list.\n- Search across note titles and content in real time.\n- Filter notes by Study, Project, Idea, Personal, or Other.\n- View saved-note, pinned-note, character, and draft statistics.\n\n### Browser storage\n\n- Notes are stored under `notes-app-notes` in **localStorage**.\n- The current editor draft is stored under `notes-app-draft` in **sessionStorage**.\n- The selected theme is stored under `notes-app-theme`.\n- Notes are stored as an array of JavaScript objects converted with `JSON.stringify()`.\n- Stored note data is restored with `JSON.parse()`.\n\n### Learning playground\n\nThe application also includes interactive areas for experimenting with:\n\n- `localStorage.setItem()` / `getItem()` / `removeItem()`;\n- `sessionStorage` using the same API;\n- viewing all keys and values;\n- JSON formatting and parsing;\n- saving a JSON object directly to localStorage.\n\n### Data portability\n\nNotes can be exported as a formatted `.json` file and imported later. Imported data is validated before it replaces the current note collection.\n\n## Project structure\n\n```text\nAssignment-1-Notes-App/\n├── index.html      # Page structure and application UI\n├── style.css       # Responsive design, light/dark themes\n├── script.js       # Notes logic, storage, JSON and interactions\n└── README.md       # Project documentation\n```\n\n## How to run\n\n1. Download or clone the repository.\n2. Open the `Assignment-1-Notes-App` folder.\n3. Open `index.html` in a modern browser.\n4. Create a note and refresh the page — the saved note remains available.\n\nFor a more development-friendly setup, open the folder in VS Code and use a local static server such as Live Server. The project does **not** require `npm install` or a database.\n\n## How the storage flow works\n\nWhen a note is saved, the application first creates a normal JavaScript object:\n\n```js\n{\n  id: \"...\",\n  title: \"Web Storage revision\",\n  category: \"Study\",\n  content: \"Revise JSON.stringify and JSON.parse\",\n  pinned: false,\n  createdAt: \"...\",\n  updatedAt: \"...\"\n}\n```\n\nThe notes array is converted to a string and saved:\n\n```js\nlocalStorage.setItem(\"notes-app-notes\", JSON.stringify(notes));\n```\n\nWhen the page loads, the stored string is converted back to an array:\n\n```js\nconst notes = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\"notes-app-notes\"));\n```\n\nThe editor draft follows the same JSON process but uses `sessionStorage`, making it suitable for temporary state.\n\n## localStorage vs sessionStorage\n\n| Feature | localStorage | sessionStorage |\n|---|---|---|\n| Persists after refresh | Yes | Yes |\n| Persists after browser/tab session ends | Yes, until deleted | No |\n| API | `setItem`, `getItem`, `removeItem`, `clear` | Same |\n| Stored value type | String | String |\n| Best suited for | Notes, preferences, persistent state | Drafts, temporary state |\n\n> Storage capacity and exact session behavior are browser-dependent. Web Storage should not be used for passwords, authentication secrets, or other sensitive information.\n\n## JSON in this project\n\nWeb Storage stores strings. Saving a JavaScript object directly would coerce it to an unhelpful string such as `[object Object]`. JSON solves this problem:\n\n```js\nconst user = { name: \"Alice\", age: 25 };\nlocalStorage.setItem(\"user\", JSON.stringify(user));\n\nconst savedUser = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\"user\"));\nconsole.log(savedUser.name); // Alice\n```\n\nThe project uses a safe parsing helper so malformed or missing stored data does not break the interface.\n\n## Testing checklist\n\n- [ ] Create a note and refresh the page; the note should remain.\n- [ ] Edit a note and confirm the card updates.\n- [ ] Pin a note and confirm it moves above unpinned notes.\n- [ ] Search and filter the note collection.\n- [ ] Start typing a draft, refresh the same tab, and verify it is restored.\n- [ ] Try the localStorage and sessionStorage playgrounds.\n- [ ] Enter valid and invalid JSON in the JSON lab.\n- [ ] Export notes, then import the generated JSON file.\n- [ ] Toggle dark mode and refresh to confirm the preference persists.\n- [ ] Resize the browser window and verify the responsive layout.\n\n## Concepts covered\n\n`Web Storage API` · `localStorage` · `sessionStorage` · `JSON.stringify()` · `JSON.parse()` · CRUD · DOM manipulation · event handling · responsive CSS · data import/export · persistent UI state\n\n## Learning outcome\n\nAfter completing this assignment, a student should be able to explain the difference between persistent and session-scoped browser storage, store structured JavaScript data safely as JSON, retrieve and modify that data, and use those concepts to build a complete client-side application.\n\n---\n\n**Assignment 01 — Web Storage API / Final Task: Notes App**\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [📚 Back to Theory README](../README.md)\n- [🚀 Launch NoteSpace Web App](./index.html)\n", "Theory/Assignment 2/README.md": "# Assignment 2 — PostgreSQL as SQL + NoSQL: Working with JSONB\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [📚 Theory README](../README.md) · [🚀 Open Interactive UI](./index.html) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=Theory/Assignment%202/README.md)\n\nA complete Theory Assignment demonstrating how **PostgreSQL JSONB** combines relational SQL features with flexible, document-style data. The project includes a runnable SQL experiment and a responsive interactive webpage that explains the major JSONB operations visually.\n\n## Learning Objective\n\nUnderstand how PostgreSQL's `jsonb` type allows one database to support both structured relational data and flexible JSON documents, and evaluate situations in which PostgreSQL JSONB can serve needs commonly associated with MongoDB.\n\n## What this project demonstrates\n\n- A relational `users` table with a flexible `JSONB` profile column\n- Inserting documents with different structures\n- Reading JSON fields with `->` and `->>`\n- Querying nested values\n- JSON containment with `@>`\n- Searching JSON arrays\n- Testing whether a key exists with `?`\n- Updating nested JSON with `jsonb_set()`\n- Merging and removing JSON properties\n- Combining ordinary SQL conditions with JSONB conditions\n- Aggregating JSON values with SQL\n- Creating GIN and expression indexes\n- Comparing PostgreSQL JSONB with MongoDB\n\n## Project structure\n\n```text\nAssignment 2/\n├── index.html          # Interactive assignment UI\n├── styles.css          # Responsive styling and light/dark themes\n├── app.js              # Query playground interactions\n├── assignment2.sql     # Complete PostgreSQL JSONB experiment\n├── README.md           # Documentation\n└── .gitignore\n```\n\n## Run the interactive UI\n\nNo framework or installation is required. Open `index.html` directly in a browser. For a local development server, you can also run:\n\n```bash\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen visit `http://localhost:8000`.\n\n> The webpage is an educational visualization. Its displayed query results are based on the sample dataset in `assignment2.sql`; it does not connect directly to PostgreSQL.\n\n## Run the PostgreSQL experiment\n\n### Requirements\n\n- PostgreSQL 12 or later\n- `psql`, pgAdmin, DBeaver, or another PostgreSQL client\n\n### Terminal method\n\n```bash\ncreatedb assignment2_db\npsql assignment2_db -f assignment2.sql\n```\n\n### pgAdmin method\n\n1. Create a database named `assignment2_db`.\n2. Open **Query Tool** for that database.\n3. Open or paste `assignment2.sql`.\n4. Execute the script.\n5. Inspect the output of each `SELECT`, `UPDATE`, and `EXPLAIN` statement.\n\n## Database design\n\nThe experiment stores predictable fields relationally and flexible attributes as JSONB:\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE users (\n    id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    email VARCHAR(150) UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n    profile JSONB NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}'::jsonb,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP\n);\n```\n\n`id`, `name`, and `email` benefit from conventional types and constraints. `profile` can vary from user to user, making it suitable for optional attributes such as skills, preferences, interests, and portfolio information.\n\n## Important JSONB operators\n\n| Operator | Purpose | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `->` | Return a JSON value | `profile->'skills'` |\n| `->>` | Return a JSON value as text | `profile->>'city'` |\n| `@>` | Test JSON containment | `profile @> '{\"city\":\"Dehradun\"}'` |\n| `?` | Check for a top-level key | `profile ? 'github'` |\n| `||` | Merge JSON objects | `profile || '{\"verified\":true}'` |\n| `-` | Remove a key | `profile - 'portfolio'` |\n\n## Example queries\n\n### Extract a field\n\n```sql\nSELECT name, profile->>'city' AS city\nFROM users;\n```\n\n### Query a nested field\n\n```sql\nSELECT name\nFROM users\nWHERE profile->'preferences'->>'theme' = 'dark';\n```\n\n### Document-style containment\n\n```sql\nSELECT name\nFROM users\nWHERE profile @> '{\"city\":\"Dehradun\"}'::jsonb;\n```\n\n### Search inside an array\n\n```sql\nSELECT name\nFROM users\nWHERE profile->'skills' @> '[\"PostgreSQL\"]'::jsonb;\n```\n\n### Update a nested value\n\n```sql\nUPDATE users\nSET profile = jsonb_set(\n    profile,\n    '{preferences,theme}',\n    '\"light\"'::jsonb,\n    true\n)\nWHERE email = 'aarav@example.com';\n```\n\n## JSONB indexing\n\nA general GIN index can improve many containment and key searches:\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_users_profile_gin\nON users USING GIN (profile);\n```\n\nA frequently queried JSON property can also receive an expression index:\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_users_profile_city\nON users ((profile->>'city'));\n```\n\n`EXPLAIN` can then be used to inspect PostgreSQL's query plan. With a tiny teaching dataset PostgreSQL may still choose a sequential scan because reading the whole table is inexpensive; indexes become more meaningful as data volume grows.\n\n## PostgreSQL JSONB vs MongoDB\n\n| Area | PostgreSQL JSONB | MongoDB |\n|---|---|---|\n| Primary model | Relational with document support | Document-oriented |\n| Document format | JSONB | BSON |\n| Schema flexibility | Flexible inside JSONB | Flexible documents |\n| Joins | Native SQL joins | `$lookup` / document modeling |\n| Constraints | Rich relational constraints | Validation and indexes |\n| Transactions | ACID transactions | ACID transactions supported |\n| Document indexing | GIN and expression indexes | Field, compound and other indexes |\n| Query style | SQL + JSON operators/functions | MongoDB Query API / aggregation pipeline |\n\n## When PostgreSQL JSONB is useful\n\nJSONB is particularly useful when an application has a stable relational core but also needs flexible attributes. Examples include user preferences, product metadata, event payloads, configuration, API responses, and optional profile fields. Keeping these alongside relational data can simplify architecture when the application also relies on joins, constraints, transactions, reporting, and SQL tooling.\n\nMongoDB can be a natural choice when the application is designed primarily around documents and its data model, operational tooling, and query patterns are already centered on MongoDB. The appropriate choice depends on the application's data relationships, access patterns, scaling requirements, and team expertise.\n\n## UI features\n\nThe included webpage adds a polished presentation layer for the assignment with a responsive layout, dark/light theme toggle, interactive JSONB query playground, copy-to-clipboard SQL, operator cards, PostgreSQL-vs-MongoDB comparison, and mobile-friendly styling. It uses only HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript, so there are no npm dependencies.\n\n## Key takeaway\n\nPostgreSQL remains a relational database when JSONB is used. JSONB extends the relational model by allowing flexible documents to live beside conventional typed columns. A practical design is to keep stable, important attributes in normal columns and use JSONB where genuine schema flexibility is valuable.\n\n## Assignment summary\n\n**Topic:** PostgreSQL as SQL + NoSQL — Working with JSONB  \n**Core technology:** PostgreSQL / JSONB  \n**Supporting UI:** HTML, CSS, JavaScript  \n**Outcome:** Demonstrates storage, querying, updates, indexing, relational/document integration, and a balanced comparison with MongoDB.\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [📚 Back to Theory README](../README.md)\n- [🚀 Launch JSONB Interactive UI](./index.html)\n", "Theory/JWT-Authentication-Lab/README.md": "# JWT Authentication Lab - Student Management API\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [📚 Theory README](../README.md) · [📖 Lecture Notes](./docs/LECTURE_NOTES.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=Theory/JWT-Authentication-Lab/README.md)\n\nA GitHub-ready **FastAPI** project based on **Lecture 20: Authentication Mechanisms - Basic Auth, Token-based Authentication, JWT**. It turns the lecture concepts and lab exercise into a working Student Management API with password hashing, JWT login, protected routes, token expiry, and authorization.\n\n> **Course Outcome (CO3):** Implement secure authentication mechanisms to protect API endpoints.\n\n## What this project demonstrates\n\nThe lecture starts with an important distinction: **authentication** asks *\"Who are you?\"*, while **authorization** asks *\"What can you do?\"*. This project implements both. A user authenticates with email/password and receives a JWT; protected routes validate that JWT; and the delete route performs an additional permission check.\n\n### Lecture-to-project mapping\n\n| Lecture topic | Implementation in this repository |\n|---|---|\n| Authentication vs Authorization | Login verifies identity; delete route checks permission |\n| HTTP Basic Auth | Explained in `docs/LECTURE_NOTES.md` for comparison |\n| Token-based Authentication | Credentials are exchanged for a token after login |\n| JWT Structure | Header + Payload + Signature |\n| JWT Claims | `sub`, `exp`, `iat`, `iss`, plus `email` and `role` |\n| Password Security | Passwords are hashed with bcrypt through Passlib |\n| Token Expiration | Access tokens expire after 30 minutes |\n| Protected API | `/protected` and `/me` require a Bearer token |\n| Authorization | Admin or account owner can delete a student |\n| API Testing | cURL examples and Swagger UI workflow included |\n\n## Authentication vs Authorization\n\n| Concept | Question | Example in this project |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Authentication** | Who are you? | Login using email and password |\n| **Authorization** | What can you do? | Admin can delete any student; a student can delete only themself |\n\nThe complete flow is:\n\n```text\nRegister -> Password is hashed -> Login -> Credentials verified\n   -> JWT generated -> Client stores token -> Bearer token sent\n   -> JWT verified -> User loaded -> Permission checked -> Response\n```\n\n## Basic Auth, Token Auth and JWT\n\nHTTP Basic Authentication sends encoded credentials with each request:\n\n```text\nAuthorization: Basic base64(username:password)\n```\n\nBase64 is **not encryption**, so Basic Auth must not be treated as secure without HTTPS. Token authentication improves the flow by exchanging credentials once for a token. JWT is a popular signed token format that can carry claims and expiration information.\n\n| Feature | Basic Auth | Token-based Auth | JWT |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Credentials sent on every request | Yes | No | No |\n| Expiration | Not inherent | Can support it | Built-in `exp` claim |\n| Claims/user metadata | No | Depends on design | Yes |\n| Signed integrity check | No | Depends on design | Yes |\n| Suitable for stateless APIs | Limited | Yes | Yes |\n\n## JWT Structure\n\nA JSON Web Token contains three dot-separated parts:\n\n```text\nxxxxx.yyyyy.zzzzz\nHEADER.PAYLOAD.SIGNATURE\n```\n\n**Header** describes the token type and signing algorithm. **Payload** contains claims. **Signature** allows the API to verify that the header/payload have not been altered.\n\nExample conceptual payload:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"sub\": \"1\",\n  \"email\": \"aarav@upes.ac.in\",\n  \"role\": \"student\",\n  \"exp\": \"<expiration timestamp>\",\n  \"iat\": \"<issued-at timestamp>\",\n  \"iss\": \"student-api\"\n}\n```\n\nJWT payloads are encoded, **not encrypted**. Never place passwords or other secrets inside them.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```text\nJWT-Authentication-Lab/\n├── main.py                 # FastAPI routes and request/response models\n├── auth.py                 # Password hashing + JWT creation/validation\n├── models.py               # SQLAlchemy Student model\n├── database.py             # SQLite/SQLAlchemy setup\n├── requirements.txt        # Python dependencies\n├── .env.example            # Secret-key example\n├── .gitignore              # Files Git should ignore\n├── docs/\n│   └── LECTURE_NOTES.md    # Theory from the supplied Lecture 20 screenshots\n└── README.md\n```\n\n## API Endpoints\n\n| Method | Endpoint | Auth required? | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `GET` | `/` | No | API status |\n| `POST` | `/register` | No | Register a new student |\n| `POST` | `/students` | No | Lecture-compatible registration alias |\n| `POST` | `/login` | No | Verify credentials and issue JWT |\n| `GET` | `/protected` | Yes | Demonstrate authenticated access |\n| `GET` | `/me` | Yes | Return the authenticated student |\n| `DELETE` | `/students/{student_id}` | Yes | Admin or same student may delete |\n\n## Installation and Setup\n\n### 1. Open the project\n\n```bash\ncd JWT-Authentication-Lab\n```\n\n### 2. Create a virtual environment\n\n```bash\npython3 -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\n```\n\nWindows PowerShell:\n\n```powershell\n.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1\n```\n\n### 3. Install dependencies\n\nThe lecture specifically uses `python-jose`, `passlib[bcrypt]`, and `python-multipart`; the project also includes FastAPI, Uvicorn, SQLAlchemy, and email validation.\n\n```bash\npip install -r requirements.txt\n```\n\n### 4. Configure the JWT secret\n\nmacOS/Linux:\n\n```bash\nexport SECRET_KEY=\"replace-this-with-a-long-random-secret\"\n```\n\nThe fallback key in `auth.py` exists only so the classroom demo can run immediately. **Never use a hard-coded development secret in production.**\n\n### 5. Run the API\n\n```bash\nuvicorn main:app --reload\n```\n\nThen open Swagger UI:\n\n```text\nhttp://127.0.0.1:8000/docs\n```\n\nThe SQLite database `students.db` is created automatically on first run.\n\n## Testing the Complete JWT Flow\n\n### Step 1 - Register a student\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:8000/register \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\n    \"name\": \"Aarav\",\n    \"email\": \"aarav@upes.ac.in\",\n    \"branch\": \"CSE\",\n    \"password\": \"securepassword123\"\n  }'\n```\n\nThe password is **hashed before storage**. The API never returns the password hash.\n\n### Step 2 - Login\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:8000/login \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"email\":\"aarav@upes.ac.in\",\"password\":\"securepassword123\"}'\n```\n\nExample response:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"access_token\": \"eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIs...\",\n  \"token_type\": \"bearer\",\n  \"expires_in_minutes\": 30\n}\n```\n\n### Step 3 - Access a protected route\n\n```bash\ncurl http://localhost:8000/protected \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_JWT_TOKEN\"\n```\n\nExample response:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"message\": \"Hello, Aarav!\",\n  \"user_id\": 1,\n  \"email\": \"aarav@upes.ac.in\",\n  \"role\": \"student\"\n}\n```\n\n### Step 4 - Try without a token\n\n```bash\ncurl http://localhost:8000/protected\n```\n\nThe request is rejected because the route depends on an authenticated user.\n\n### Step 5 - Test authorization\n\n```bash\ncurl -X DELETE http://localhost:8000/students/1 \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_JWT_TOKEN\"\n```\n\nThe endpoint allows deletion only when the authenticated user is an **admin** or the student whose ID appears in the URL. An authenticated user without permission receives **403 Forbidden**.\n\n## How the Code Works\n\n### Password hashing\n\n`get_password_hash()` converts the plaintext password into a bcrypt hash before database storage. During login, `verify_password()` compares the submitted password against the stored hash. Plaintext passwords are never stored.\n\n### Token creation\n\n`create_access_token()` copies the supplied claims, adds `exp`, `iat`, and `iss`, then signs the token using `HS256` and `SECRET_KEY`.\n\n### Token validation\n\n`OAuth2PasswordBearer` extracts the Bearer token. `decode_token()` verifies the JWT signature, issuer, and expiration. `get_current_user()` reads `sub`, converts it to a student ID, and loads that student from the database.\n\n### Authorization\n\nAuthentication alone is not enough for every operation. The delete route checks:\n\n```python\nif current_user.role != \"admin\" and current_user.id != student_id:\n    # 403 Forbidden\n```\n\nThis directly demonstrates the lecture's difference between identity verification and permission checking.\n\n## HTTP Status Codes Used\n\n| Status | Meaning in this lab |\n|---|---|\n| `201 Created` | Student registered successfully |\n| `204 No Content` | Student deleted successfully |\n| `401 Unauthorized` | Login failed or token is missing/invalid/expired |\n| `403 Forbidden` | Authenticated, but not allowed to perform the action |\n| `404 Not Found` | Student does not exist |\n| `409 Conflict` | Email is already registered |\n\n## Token Storage Concepts\n\nThe lecture compares memory, `localStorage`, and HttpOnly cookies. This backend returns a Bearer token and leaves client storage to the frontend. For browser applications, token storage is a security design decision: `localStorage` is accessible to JavaScript and therefore exposed if XSS occurs; Secure HttpOnly cookies reduce JavaScript access but require appropriate CSRF defenses.\n\n## Security Best Practices\n\nThe implementation and lecture emphasize:\n\n- Use **HTTPS** so credentials and tokens are encrypted in transit.\n- Keep access tokens **short-lived**; this lab uses **30 minutes**.\n- Use refresh-token designs when longer sessions are needed.\n- Hash passwords; never store plaintext passwords.\n- Keep signing secrets in environment variables or a secret manager.\n- Verify JWT signature, issuer, and expiration on protected requests.\n- Do not place passwords or secrets in JWT payloads.\n- Rate-limit login attempts to reduce brute-force attacks.\n- Configure CORS only for trusted origins.\n- Choose token storage with XSS and CSRF risks in mind.\n- Public registration in this project always creates the `student` role so a client cannot self-register as an admin.\n\n## Lab Exercise Checklist\n\n- [x] Implement JWT authentication for the Student Management API.\n- [x] Add a password field and store only its hash.\n- [x] Create `/register` and `/login` endpoints.\n- [x] Protect API routes using Bearer JWT authentication.\n- [x] Secure `DELETE /students/{id}` for admins or the student themself.\n- [x] Register a student and login to obtain a JWT.\n- [x] Access a protected route with a token.\n- [x] Reject protected access without a valid token.\n- [x] Implement and validate token expiration.\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n**Authentication verifies identity; authorization controls permissions.** Basic Auth is easy to understand but repeatedly sends credentials. Token-based authentication avoids resending credentials after login. JWT provides a compact signed format containing claims such as subject and expiration. Secure authentication still depends on HTTPS, password hashing, short token lifetimes, safe secret management, careful token storage, and authorization checks.\n\nFor the expanded theory corresponding to the lecture screenshots, read [`docs/LECTURE_NOTES.md`](docs/LECTURE_NOTES.md).\n\n## Technologies\n\n`Python` · `FastAPI` · `SQLAlchemy` · `SQLite` · `python-jose` · `JWT` · `Passlib` · `bcrypt` · `Pydantic`\n\n## Academic Context\n\n**Lecture 20 - Authentication Mechanisms: Basic Auth, Token-based Auth, JWT**  \n**Unit:** Unit 2 - Database Management  \n**Course Outcome:** CO3  \n**Bloom's Level:** Apply  \n**Application:** API security, user identity verification, secure backend services\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [📚 Back to Theory README](../README.md)\n- [📖 Lecture 20 Notes](./docs/LECTURE_NOTES.md)\n", "Theory/JWT-Authentication-Lab/docs/LECTURE_NOTES.md": "# Lecture 20 Notes - Authentication Mechanisms\n\n## 1. Authentication and Authorization\n\n**Authentication** answers **\"Who are you?\"** and verifies identity, for example by checking an email and password.\n\n**Authorization** answers **\"What can you do?\"** and checks permissions after authentication. In this lab, a student may delete their own account while an admin may delete any student account.\n\n## 2. HTTP Basic Authentication\n\nBasic Authentication sends an `Authorization` header containing a Base64 representation of `username:password`.\n\n```text\nAuthorization: Basic base64(username:password)\n```\n\nFlow: client sends credentials -> server decodes/verifies -> server returns data or `401 Unauthorized`.\n\nAdvantages: simple and widely supported. Limitations: credentials are sent with every request, Base64 is encoding rather than encryption, browser credential caching complicates logout, and Basic Auth has no built-in token expiry. HTTPS is essential.\n\n## 3. Token-based Authentication\n\nInstead of sending credentials repeatedly, the user logs in once and receives a token. Later requests send the token to the API. The API validates the token before returning protected data.\n\nTypical flow: Login -> token issued -> request + token -> token validated -> database/resource accessed -> response.\n\n### Client-side token storage discussed in the lecture\n\n| Storage | Main consideration | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Memory | Lost on refresh | SPA with a refresh-token design |\n| localStorage | Accessible to JavaScript; XSS exposure | Simple demos, not preferred for sensitive production auth |\n| Secure HttpOnly cookie | Not readable by JavaScript; requires CSRF-aware design | Common web-app option |\n\n## 4. JWT - JSON Web Token\n\nJWT is a compact, URL-safe signed token format. A JWT has three dot-separated sections:\n\n```text\nxxxxx.yyyyy.zzzzz\nHEADER.PAYLOAD.SIGNATURE\n```\n\n- **Header:** algorithm and token type, e.g. `HS256` and `JWT`.\n- **Payload:** claims describing the subject/user and token metadata.\n- **Signature:** verifies integrity and detects tampering.\n\nJWT payloads are encoded, not encrypted. Never place passwords or secrets in the payload.\n\n## 5. JWT Claims\n\nRegistered claims highlighted in the lecture include:\n\n| Claim | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `sub` | Subject, usually the user ID |\n| `exp` | Expiration time |\n| `iat` | Issued-at time |\n| `iss` | Issuer |\n\nThis project also uses custom claims such as `email` and `role`.\n\n## 6. JWT Authentication Flow\n\n1. User submits email and password.\n2. Server verifies credentials.\n3. Server generates a signed JWT.\n4. Server returns the JWT to the client.\n5. Client sends `Authorization: Bearer <token>` with protected requests.\n6. Server verifies signature, issuer, and expiration.\n7. Server reads `sub`, loads the user, applies authorization rules, and returns the response.\n\n## 7. Security Best Practices\n\nUse HTTPS; keep access tokens short-lived (the lecture recommends roughly 15-30 minutes); use refresh tokens for longer sessions; store secrets outside source code; hash passwords using a suitable password hashing algorithm; validate signatures and expiration; rate-limit login attempts; configure CORS carefully; and choose browser token storage with XSS/CSRF risks in mind.\n\n## 8. Lab Exercise Mapping\n\nThe supplied project implements the lecture exercise: JWT authentication for the Student Management API, hashed passwords, `/register` and `/login`, a protected route, an admin-or-self rule for `DELETE /students/{id}`, `401` behavior for invalid/missing tokens, and token expiration.\n", "Theory/Theory 1/README.md": "# Theory 1 — Backend Development Practice Project\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [📚 Theory README](../README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=Theory/Theory%201/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\nA small learning project containing **two independent backend examples**:\n\n1. **Node.js + Express + EJS** - a simple Student Management website and JSON API.\n2. **Python + Flask** - a minimal server demonstrating HTML and JSON responses.\n\nThe project is useful for understanding backend fundamentals such as routing, server-side rendering, static files, JSON APIs, and running development servers.\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```text\nTheory/\n├── flask server/\n│   ├── main.py            # Flask application\n│   ├── Pipfile            # Optional Pipenv configuration\n│   └── Pipfile.lock\n├── public/\n│   └── style.css          # Express/EJS website styling\n├── views/\n│   ├── home.ejs           # Express home page\n│   └── students.ejs       # Student listing page\n├── .gitignore\n├── package.json           # Node dependencies and scripts\n├── package-lock.json\n├── requirements.txt       # Python dependency list\n├── server.js              # Express application\n└── README.md\n```\n\n## What the Express Application Does\n\nThe Express application runs on port **3000** by default and contains these routes:\n\n| Route | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `/` | Displays the project home page |\n| `/students` | Renders the sample student list with EJS |\n| `/api/students` | Returns the student list as JSON |\n\nThe sample data is currently stored in memory inside `server.js`. No database is required.\n\n## What the Flask Application Does\n\nThe Flask example runs on port **5000** and demonstrates simple backend responses:\n\n| Route | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `/` | Shows a basic Flask landing page |\n| `/data` | Returns sample data as JSON |\n| `/html` | Displays the same data as server-generated HTML |\n\n## Requirements\n\nFor the Express example:\n\n- Node.js\n- npm\n\nFor the Flask example:\n\n- Python 3\n- pip\n\n## Run the Express + EJS Application\n\nFrom the `Theory` directory, install dependencies:\n\n```bash\nnpm install\n```\n\nStart the server:\n\n```bash\nnpm start\n```\n\nFor automatic restart during development:\n\n```bash\nnpm run dev\n```\n\nThen open:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:3000\n```\n\nUseful URLs:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:3000/students\nhttp://localhost:3000/api/students\n```\n\n## Run the Flask Application\n\nFrom the `Theory` directory, create a virtual environment if desired:\n\n```bash\npython3 -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\n```\n\nInstall Flask:\n\n```bash\npip install -r requirements.txt\n```\n\nRun the application:\n\n```bash\npython3 \"flask server/main.py\"\n```\n\nThen open:\n\n```text\nhttp://127.0.0.1:5000\n```\n\nUseful URLs:\n\n```text\nhttp://127.0.0.1:5000/data\nhttp://127.0.0.1:5000/html\n```\n\n## Concepts Demonstrated\n\n- Creating backend servers\n- HTTP routing\n- Express middleware\n- EJS server-side templates\n- Serving static CSS files\n- Returning JSON from an API endpoint\n- Flask routes\n- Flask `jsonify`\n- Basic separation of views and backend logic\n- Environment-based server ports in Node.js\n- Basic 404 handling\n\n## Improvements Made\n\nThe original project was functional as a basic Express demonstration, but it also contained duplicated files and an incomplete Flask example. The updated version includes:\n\n- A cleaner project structure\n- Duplicate Flask files removed\n- macOS metadata removed\n- Fixed Flask code that previously referenced an undefined `data` variable\n- Proper JSON output from Flask\n- A new Express JSON API endpoint\n- Responsive EJS pages with shared CSS styling\n- Improved page metadata and mobile support\n- Configurable Express port through the `PORT` environment variable\n- Basic Express 404 handling\n- A repository-wide `.gitignore`\n- Simplified Python requirements\n- This complete README\n\n## Possible Next Steps\n\nThis is intentionally still a beginner-friendly project. It can later be extended with:\n\n- MongoDB or another database\n- Add/Edit/Delete student operations\n- HTML forms and validation\n- REST API `POST`, `PUT`, and `DELETE` routes\n- Authentication\n- Error-handling middleware\n- Automated tests\n- Environment variables with `.env`\n\n## Notes\n\nThe Express and Flask examples are **separate servers**. You do not need to run both at the same time. Use whichever example you are studying.\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [📚 Back to Theory README](../README.md)\n- [🌐 Open Website Portal](../../index.html)\n", "Theory/Unit 3/README.md": "# Unit 3 — Django Student System\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [📚 Theory README](../README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=Theory/Unit%203/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\nTheory Unit 3 introduces **Django** project and application architecture through a minimal `student_system` web service.\n\n---\n\n## 📂 Structure\n\n```text\nUnit 3/\n├── Django/\n│   └── student_system/\n│       ├── manage.py\n│       ├── student_system/\n│       │   ├── settings.py\n│       │   ├── urls.py\n│       │   ├── asgi.py\n│       │   └── wsgi.py\n│       └── students/\n│           ├── views.py\n│           ├── models.py\n│           ├── admin.py\n│           └── tests.py\n└── README.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🧭 Implemented Endpoints\n\n| Method | Route | View Function | Response |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `GET` | `/` | `students.views.index` | Plain HTTP welcome response |\n| `GET` | `/json/` | `students.views.json_response` | JSON API status payload (`message`, `version`, `status`) |\n| `GET` | `/admin/` | Django Admin | Built-in Django administrative portal |\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ How to Run\n\n```bash\ncd \"Theory/Unit 3/Django/student_system\"\npython3 -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npip install django\npython manage.py migrate\npython manage.py runserver\n```\n\nThen visit:\n- `http://127.0.0.1:8000/`\n- `http://127.0.0.1:8000/json/`\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [📚 Back to Theory README](../README.md)\n- [✅ Related: Django To-Do App Lab](../../LABS/django_todo_app/README.md)\n\n**Made by Arnav Daftuar** · Backend Development (18722)\n", "Theory/Unit 4/README.md": "# Unit 4 — Docker & Containerized Express\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [📚 Theory README](../README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=Theory/Unit%204/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\nTheory Unit 4 covers **Docker containerization fundamentals** across two hands-on folders: preserving changes inside containers and containerizing a Node.js Express API.\n\n---\n\n## 📂 Structure\n\n```text\nUnit 4/\n├── Preserve Changes inside a Container/\n│   ├── Hello.java\n│   └── Hello.class\n├── express-project/\n│   ├── Dockerfile\n│   ├── app.js\n│   └── package.json\n└── README.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## 1. Preserve Changes inside a Container\n\nDemonstrates compiling and running `Hello.java` inside a container (`Hello from Docker container!`) and persisting container filesystem state into a reusable image.\n\n```bash\njavac Hello.java\njava Hello\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Containerized Express Project (`express-project/`)\n\nA lightweight Express server configured to bind to `0.0.0.0:3000` inside a Docker container.\n\n### Endpoints\n\n| Method | Route | Response |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `GET` | `/` | `\"Hello! Express is running inside Docker.\"` |\n| `GET` | `/api/students` | JSON array of sample students |\n\n### Run with Docker\n\n```bash\ncd \"Theory/Unit 4/express-project\"\ndocker build -t express-unit4 .\ndocker run -p 3000:3000 express-unit4\n```\n\n### Run Locally with Node.js\n\n```bash\ncd \"Theory/Unit 4/express-project\"\nnpm install\nnode app.js\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [📚 Back to Theory README](../README.md)\n- [🛡️ Related: Lab Test-9 Docker & Security](../../LABS/Lab%20Test-9%20Experiment/README.md)\n\n**Made by Arnav Daftuar** · Backend Development (18722)\n", "Applications/README.md": "# 🖥️ Applications — Backend Development\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../README.md) · [🧪 LABS](../LABS/README.md) · [📚 Theory](../Theory/README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=Applications/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\nThis directory contains integrated, full-stack backend applications built during the **Backend Development (18722)** course.\n\n---\n\n## 📦 Applications Index\n\n| Application | Stack | Key Capabilities | Folder | Documentation |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **CMS Lab** | Node.js, Express.js, EJS, MongoDB | Blog post creation, listing, detail view, validation, MongoDB + demo mode fallback | [Open Project](./cms-lab/) | [cms-lab/README.md](./cms-lab/README.md) |\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../README.md)\n- [🧪 LABS README](../LABS/README.md)\n- [📚 Theory README](../Theory/README.md)\n- [🌐 Open Interactive Website Portal](../index.html)\n\n---\n\n**Made by Arnav Daftuar** · Backend Development Applications\n", "Applications/cms-lab/README.md": "# CMS Lab\n\n> **Navigation:** [🏠 Main README](../../README.md) · [🖥️ Applications README](../README.md) · [🌐 Website View](https://iambolt7.github.io/Backend.Develepment.18722/#doc=Applications/cms-lab/README.md) · [💻 GitHub Repo](https://github.com/IamBolt7/Backend.Develepment.18722)\n\nA simple, responsive **Content Management System (CMS)** for creating and reading blog posts. The project is built with **Node.js, Express.js, EJS, and MongoDB** and is designed as a practical backend-development lab project.\n\nThe application can run in two modes:\n\n- **MongoDB mode** — posts are stored permanently in MongoDB.\n- **Demo mode** — if MongoDB is unavailable, the application still starts and uses temporary in-memory sample posts.\n\nThis makes the project easy to demonstrate on `localhost:3000` while still showing real database integration when MongoDB is available.\n\n---\n\n## Features\n\n- View all published blog posts.\n- Open an individual post and read its complete content.\n- Create new blog posts through a web form.\n- Validate the title, author, and content before creating a post.\n- Store posts in MongoDB when the database is available.\n- Automatically fall back to in-memory demo data if MongoDB is not running.\n- Automatically add sample blog posts to an empty MongoDB collection.\n- Responsive user interface for desktop, tablet, and mobile screens.\n- Custom 404 page for invalid URLs or missing posts.\n- Clean Express routing and EJS-based server-side rendering.\n\n---\n\n## Technologies Used\n\n| Technology | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| Node.js | JavaScript runtime used to run the backend server |\n| Express.js | Web framework used for routing and request handling |\n| EJS | Template engine used to generate dynamic HTML pages |\n| MongoDB | Database used to store blog posts permanently |\n| MongoDB Node.js Driver | Connects the Express application to MongoDB |\n| HTML5 | Provides the structure of the web pages |\n| CSS3 | Provides styling and responsive design |\n\n---\n\n## Project Structure\n\n```text\ncms-lab/\n├── app.js\n├── package.json\n├── package-lock.json\n├── README.md\n├── screenshot.jpg\n├── public/\n│   └── style.css\n└── views/\n    ├── posts.ejs\n    ├── post.ejs\n    ├── new-post.ejs\n    └── not-found.ejs\n```\n\n### Important Files\n\n**`app.js`**  \nContains the Express server, routes, MongoDB connection, form validation, sample data, error handling, and application startup logic.\n\n**`public/style.css`**  \nContains the complete styling and responsive layout of the application.\n\n**`views/posts.ejs`**  \nDisplays the main blog page containing the list of posts.\n\n**`views/post.ejs`**  \nDisplays the complete content of one selected blog post.\n\n**`views/new-post.ejs`**  \nContains the form used to create a new post and displays validation errors when required fields are missing.\n\n**`views/not-found.ejs`**  \nDisplays the custom 404 page when a route or post cannot be found.\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisites\n\nBefore running the project, make sure **Node.js** and **npm** are installed.\n\nCheck them from Terminal:\n\n```bash\nnode --version\nnpm --version\n```\n\nIf both commands display version numbers, Node.js and npm are ready.\n\nMongoDB is **optional** for simply running and demonstrating this version of the project. It is required only if you want posts to remain saved after restarting the server.\n\n---\n\n## Installation\n\n### 1. Extract the project\n\nExtract `cms-lab.zip` to a folder on your computer.\n\n### 2. Open Terminal in the project folder\n\nFor example, on macOS:\n\n```bash\ncd /path/to/cms-lab\n```\n\nReplace `/path/to/cms-lab` with the actual location of the extracted project.\n\nA simple alternative on macOS is to type `cd ` in Terminal and drag the `cms-lab` folder into the Terminal window.\n\n### 3. Install dependencies\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\nnpm install\n```\n\nThis installs the packages listed in `package.json`, including Express, EJS, and the MongoDB driver.\n\n### 4. Start the application\n\nRun:\n\n```bash\nnpm start\n```\n\nThe application uses port **3000** by default.\n\nWhen it starts successfully, Terminal displays a message similar to:\n\n```text\nCMS Lab is running at http://localhost:3000\n```\n\n### 5. Open the application\n\nOpen a browser and visit:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:3000\n```\n\nThe root URL automatically redirects to `/posts`.\n\n---\n\n## Running Without MongoDB — Demo Mode\n\nMongoDB does **not** have to be running for the application to open.\n\nIf the application cannot connect to MongoDB, it automatically switches to **demo mode** and displays six sample blog posts stored temporarily in memory.\n\nTerminal will show messages similar to:\n\n```text\nMongoDB is unavailable - running in demo mode with in-memory posts.\nStart MongoDB and restart the app when you want persistent posts.\nCMS Lab is running at http://localhost:3000\n```\n\nYou can still:\n\n- Browse all sample posts.\n- Open individual posts.\n- Create new posts.\n- Test form validation.\n- Demonstrate the user interface.\n\n### Important limitation of demo mode\n\nPosts created in demo mode are stored only in the server's memory. They will disappear when the Node.js server is stopped or restarted.\n\nUse MongoDB mode if you need permanent data storage.\n\n---\n\n## Running With MongoDB — Persistent Mode\n\nBy default, the application tries to connect to:\n\n```text\nmongodb://127.0.0.1:27017\n```\n\nThe database and collection used by the application are:\n\n```text\nDatabase:   cms_lab\nCollection: posts\n```\n\nWhen MongoDB connects successfully, Terminal displays:\n\n```text\nConnected to MongoDB\n```\n\nIf the `posts` collection is empty, the application automatically inserts the included sample posts. It checks the collection first, so these starter posts are not inserted repeatedly every time the server starts.\n\nNew posts created through the application are then stored permanently in MongoDB.\n\n---\n\n## Using a Different MongoDB Connection\n\nThe application reads a custom MongoDB connection string from the `MONGO_URL` environment variable.\n\nFor example:\n\n```bash\nMONGO_URL=\"mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017\" npm start\n```\n\nYou can also use a compatible remote MongoDB connection string instead of the local URL.\n\nThe database name remains `cms_lab` unless it is changed in `app.js`.\n\n---\n\n## Application Routes\n\n| Method | Route | Description |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GET | `/` | Redirects the user to `/posts` |\n| GET | `/posts` | Displays all blog posts |\n| GET | `/posts/new` | Displays the create-post form |\n| POST | `/posts` | Validates and creates a new blog post |\n| GET | `/posts/:id` | Displays one complete blog post using its ID |\n| GET | Any unknown route | Displays the custom 404 page |\n\n---\n\n## How the Application Works\n\n### 1. Server startup\n\nWhen `npm start` is executed, Node.js runs `app.js`.\n\nThe application first attempts to connect to MongoDB. If the connection succeeds, it uses the `cms_lab` database. If the connection fails, it creates temporary in-memory copies of the sample posts and continues running normally.\n\n### 2. Viewing posts\n\nWhen the browser requests:\n\n```text\nGET /posts\n```\n\nExpress retrieves the posts from MongoDB or the in-memory array, sorts them with the newest posts first, and renders `views/posts.ejs`.\n\n### 3. Creating a post\n\nThe user opens:\n\n```text\nGET /posts/new\n```\n\nThe form accepts:\n\n- Title\n- Author\n- Content\n\nWhen the form is submitted, it sends:\n\n```text\nPOST /posts\n```\n\nThe server removes unnecessary leading/trailing whitespace and checks that all three fields contain a value.\n\nIf a field is empty, the form is displayed again with validation errors. If all values are valid, a new post is created with the current date and time.\n\n### 4. Opening one post\n\nEach post has a unique MongoDB-style `ObjectId`. Selecting a post sends a request to:\n\n```text\nGET /posts/:id\n```\n\nThe server validates the ID, finds the corresponding post, and renders `views/post.ejs`. If the ID is invalid or no matching post exists, the custom 404 page is displayed.\n\n---\n\n## Data Structure\n\nA blog post follows this general structure:\n\n```javascript\n{\n    _id: ObjectId,\n    title: \"Post title\",\n    author: \"Author name\",\n    content: \"Complete blog post content\",\n    createdAt: Date\n}\n```\n\n### Field Description\n\n| Field | Description |\n| --- | --- |\n| `_id` | Unique identifier generated using MongoDB `ObjectId` |\n| `title` | Title of the blog post |\n| `author` | Name of the post author |\n| `content` | Full text/content of the post |\n| `createdAt` | Date and time when the post was created |\n\n---\n\n## Form Validation\n\nThe server validates new posts before saving them.\n\nThe following fields are required:\n\n```text\nTitle\nAuthor\nContent\n```\n\nIf any field is empty, the server returns the form with an appropriate error instead of creating an incomplete post.\n\nValidation is performed on the backend, so it is not dependent only on browser-side HTML validation.\n\n---\n\n## Sample Blog Posts\n\nThe project contains six starter posts covering topics such as:\n\n- Node.js and Express\n- MongoDB\n- REST routes\n- EJS templates\n- Coding practices\n- Lessons from building a mini CMS\n\nThey make it possible to demonstrate the application immediately without manually creating content first.\n\nIn MongoDB mode, they are added only when the collection is empty. In demo mode, temporary copies are created each time the server starts.\n\n---\n\n## Changing the Port\n\nThe default port is:\n\n```text\n3000\n```\n\nThe server uses the `PORT` environment variable when one is provided.\n\nFor example, to run on port 4000:\n\n```bash\nPORT=4000 npm start\n```\n\nThen open:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:4000\n```\n\n---\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n### `localhost:3000` is not opening\n\nMake sure the server is actually running:\n\n```bash\nnpm start\n```\n\nKeep that Terminal window open while using the website.\n\nThen open the full address:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:3000\n```\n\nDo not try to open `localhost:3000` before starting the Node.js server.\n\n### `npm: command not found`\n\nNode.js/npm is not installed correctly. Install Node.js and reopen Terminal before running the project again.\n\n### `Cannot find module ...`\n\nThe dependencies may not have been installed. Run:\n\n```bash\nnpm install\n```\n\nThen start the application again:\n\n```bash\nnpm start\n```\n\n### MongoDB is not installed or is not running\n\nThat is acceptable for this project. The app automatically switches to demo mode and should still open on port 3000.\n\nRemember that posts created in demo mode disappear after a server restart.\n\n### Port 3000 is already in use\n\nAnother application may already be using port 3000. Start this project on another port:\n\n```bash\nPORT=4000 npm start\n```\n\nThen visit:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:4000\n```\n\n### A post URL displays the 404 page\n\nThe post may not exist, or the supplied ID may not be a valid `ObjectId`. Return to `/posts` and open a post from the available list.\n\n---\n\n## Stopping the Server\n\nReturn to the Terminal window in which the application is running and press:\n\n```text\nControl + C\n```\n\nThis stops the Node.js server.\n\nIf you were using demo mode, any posts created during that session will be cleared when the application is started again.\n\n---\n\n## Possible Future Improvements\n\nThe project can be extended with:\n\n- Edit post functionality\n- Delete post functionality\n- User registration and login\n- Admin authentication\n- Categories and tags\n- Search and filtering\n- Image uploads\n- Draft and published post states\n- Pagination\n- Comments\n- Rich-text editing\n- MongoDB Atlas deployment\n- Production deployment\n\n---\n\n## Learning Outcomes\n\nThis project demonstrates several important backend-development concepts:\n\n1. Creating a web server with Node.js and Express.\n2. Defining GET and POST routes.\n3. Handling HTML form submissions.\n4. Performing server-side input validation.\n5. Rendering dynamic pages with EJS.\n6. Serving static CSS files with Express.\n7. Connecting a Node.js application to MongoDB.\n8. Performing basic database insert and read operations.\n9. Working with MongoDB `ObjectId` values.\n10. Handling 404 and server errors.\n11. Using environment variables for configuration.\n12. Providing a fallback mode when an external service is unavailable.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nFor a quick demonstration, the complete process is:\n\n```bash\n# Open Terminal in the extracted cms-lab folder\nnpm install\nnpm start\n```\n\nThen visit:\n\n```text\nhttp://localhost:3000\n```\n\nMongoDB is optional for this quick-start workflow. If it is unavailable, the application automatically runs in demo mode.\n\n---\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**CMS Lab** is a beginner-friendly backend web application that combines Express routing, EJS templates, form processing, validation, MongoDB storage, and responsive frontend styling in one project. Its fallback demo mode makes the application easy to run for demonstrations, while MongoDB support provides persistent storage for normal use.\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Connected Navigation\n\n- [🏠 Back to Main Repository README](../../README.md)\n- [🖥️ Back to Applications README](../README.md)\n- [🌐 Open Website Portal](../../index.html)\n\n**Made by Arnav Daftuar** · Backend Development (18722)\n"};
